                    {
                        "id": "6e421e92-3866-433e-948f-9906f4746f79",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with symptoms of gastroenteritis, including vomiting, diarrhea, and bloody stools. He also has a fever, tachycardia, and hypertension, which may indicate dehydration and/or sepsis. On physical exam, he has diffuse abdominal tenderness and dry mucous membranes. Stool is guaiac positive, indicating the presence of blood in the stool.\n\nThe next day, the patient complains of lower extremity weakness and tingling, and on exam, he has diminished patellar deep tendon reflexes and lower extremity weakness. These symptoms suggest a neurological complication, such as Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome (GBS), which is a rare but serious complication of gastroenteritis.\n\nTo confirm the diagnosis of GBS, the patient would need a lumbar puncture to analyze the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). The lab findings that are most likely to be seen in this patient with GBS are:\n\nD) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count\n\nGBS is characterized by an elevated protein level in the CSF, which is caused by the immune system attacking the myelin sheath that covers the nerves. This results in nerve damage and muscle weakness. However, the cell count in the CSF is usually normal, as GBS is not an infection and does not cause an inflammatory response in the CSF.\n\nA) Gram stain positive CSF\n\nThis finding would suggest a bacterial infection of the central nervous system, such as meningitis or encephalitis. However, this is unlikely in a patient with GBS, as GBS is not caused by a bacterial infection.\n\nB) Peripheral eosinophilia\n\nThis finding would suggest an allergic or parasitic reaction, but it is not specific to GBS.\n\nC) Xanthochromia on cerebrospinal fluid analysis\n\nThis finding would suggest the presence of blood breakdown products in the CSF, which can occur in cases of subarachnoid hemorrhage or traumatic tap. However, this is not specific to GBS.\n\nE) Oligoclonal bands on cerebrospinal fluid analysis\n\nThis finding would suggest the presence of abnormal immune cells in the CSF, which can occur in cases of multiple sclerosis or other autoimmune disorders. However, this is not specific to GBS.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c0b42747-7706-40ed-9e81-49c047d11744",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
