                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step. \n\nThe patient has poor weight gain and is diaphoretic with a paucity of subcutaneous fat. This suggests that he is not getting enough calories. He is latching on and breastfeeding well, so he is probably not having trouble sucking or swallowing. \n\nThe most likely cause of poor weight gain is inadequate milk supply. This can be due to insufficient glandular tissue, poor let-down, or both. The mother's history of Graves' disease and thyroidectomy may be relevant. Graves' disease is an autoimmune disorder that can cause hyperthyroidism. Thyroidectomy is the surgical removal of all or part of the thyroid gland. The thyroid gland produces thyroid hormone, which is necessary for growth and development. A deficiency of thyroid hormone can cause poor weight gain. \n\nThe most likely cause of the patient's poor weight gain is inadequate milk supply due to a deficiency of thyroid hormone.",
