                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, the patient is unstable with a penetrating wound to the chest and a full thickness injury to the right ventricular free wall. This requires immediate surgical intervention.\n\nA) Immediate cardiac transplant is not a feasible option in this scenario as it is a complex and time-consuming procedure that requires a suitable donor heart and a stable patient.\n\nB) Watchful waiting while resuscitative fluids are initiated is not appropriate as the patient is unstable and requires immediate surgical intervention.\n\nC) Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets is a possible surgical approach for repairing the penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall. However, this approach may not be sufficient to address any other injuries that may be present.\n\nD) Needle thoracostomy over the 2nd intercostal space is not appropriate in this scenario as it is a procedure used to relieve tension pneumothorax, which is not the primary concern in this patient.\n\nE) Surgical adhesive glue is not appropriate in this scenario as it is not a definitive surgical approach and may not be effective in repairing a full thickness injury to the right ventricular free wall.\n\nTherefore, the best definitive surgical approach to take in this patient is C) Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets, with the understanding that additional surgical interventions may be necessary depending on the extent of the injuries.",
