                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has a penetrating wound to the chest that has caused a full thickness injury to the right ventricular free wall. This is a life-threatening injury that requires immediate surgical intervention. \n\nOption A, immediate cardiac transplant, is not a feasible option in this scenario. It is a complex and time-consuming procedure that is not appropriate for a patient in such critical condition. \n\nOption B, watchful waiting while resuscitative fluids are initiated, is also not appropriate. The patient is already receiving aggressive fluid resuscitation, but this alone will not address the underlying injury. \n\nOption D, needle thoracostomy over the 2nd intercostal space, is a procedure used to relieve tension pneumothorax, which is not the case in this patient. \n\nOption E, surgical adhesive glue, is not appropriate for a full thickness injury to the right ventricular free wall. \n\nTherefore, the best option is C, interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets. This is a surgical technique used to repair penetrating injuries to the heart. It involves using sutures to close the wound and pledgets to provide additional support. This approach is appropriate for this patient's injury and should be performed as soon as possible to improve the patient's chances of survival.",
