                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Immediate cardiac transplant - This is not a feasible option in this acute setting, as the patient requires immediate intervention to address the penetrating injury.\n\nB) Watchful waiting while resuscitative fluids are initiated - This patient has a life-threatening injury and requires immediate surgical intervention. Waiting is not appropriate in this case.\n\nC) Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets - This is a reasonable option for repairing the penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall. The use of sutures with supporting pledgets can help to provide a secure closure of the wound and minimize the risk of further bleeding.\n\nD) Needle thoracostomy over the 2nd intercostal space - This procedure is used to treat tension pneumothorax, which is not the primary issue in this patient. The patient has a penetrating cardiac injury that requires direct repair.\n\nE) Surgical adhesive glue - While surgical glue can be used in some cases to close wounds, it is not appropriate for a full-thickness penetrating injury to the heart. Sutures are a more secure and reliable method of closure in this case.\n\nBased on the information provided, the best definitive surgical approach for this patient is C) Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets.",
