                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nC) Acral lentiginous melanoma is the type of melanoma that is most commonly found in African-Americans and is often found on the palms of the hands, soles of the feet, and under the nails. While this patient does not have any abnormal moles or warts on her skin, her family history of metastatic melanoma and regular travel to the Carribean put her at increased risk for developing melanoma. Desmoplastic melanoma is a rare subtype that is often found on the head and neck, nodular melanoma is a rapidly growing subtype that can occur anywhere on the body, lentigo maligna melanoma is often found on sun-exposed areas of the skin in older individuals, and superficial spreading melanoma is the most common subtype overall and typically presents as an irregularly shaped mole that changes over time.",
