                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand the different types of melanoma:\n\n1. Superficial spreading melanoma: This is the most common type of melanoma, accounting for about 70% of cases. It usually appears as a flat or slightly raised discolored patch with irregular borders.\n\n2. Nodular melanoma: This type of melanoma is less common but more aggressive. It appears as a raised bump that is usually black or dark brown.\n\n3. Lentigo maligna melanoma: This type of melanoma usually occurs in older people and appears as a flat, tan or brown patch with irregular borders.\n\n4. Acral lentiginous melanoma: This type of melanoma is rare and usually occurs on the palms of the hands, soles of the feet, or under the nails.\n\n5. Desmoplastic melanoma: This type of melanoma is also rare and usually appears as a firm, flesh-colored or bluish-gray nodule.\n\nNow, let's look at the patient's characteristics and risk factors. She is African-American, which puts her at lower risk for melanoma compared to Caucasians. However, her uncle died of metastatic melanoma, which suggests a possible genetic predisposition. She also travels to the Carribean regularly, which increases her exposure to UV radiation.\n\nBased on these factors, the most likely type of melanoma for this patient is acral lentiginous melanoma (C), which is more common in people with darker skin and can occur on the palms of the hands or soles of the feet. However, it's important to note that any type of melanoma can occur in any individual, regardless of skin color or other risk factors.",
