                    },
                    {
                        "id": "4b5a7ad3-5b01-40c0-849d-5c122b2094b1",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has monoclonal gammopathy, which means there is an abnormal proliferation of a single clone of plasma cells. The bone marrow biopsy shows 6% monoclonal plasma cells, which is not a very high percentage. However, further analysis shows that class I major histocompatibility molecules are downregulated in these monoclonal plasma cells. This is important because class I major histocompatibility molecules are normally expressed on the surface of all nucleated cells and present endogenous antigens to CD8+ T lymphocytes. This allows CD8+ T lymphocytes to recognize and kill abnormal cells, such as virus-infected or cancerous cells. However, if class I major histocompatibility molecules are downregulated, CD8+ T lymphocytes cannot recognize and kill these abnormal cells.\n\nThe question asks which class of immune cells can lyse abnormal cells without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation. This rules out CD8+ T lymphocytes, which require recognition of antigens presented on class I major histocompatibility molecules. It also rules out bone marrow-derived macrophages and band neutrophils, which are phagocytic cells that require opsonization or priming to recognize and engulf abnormal cells.\n\nThis leaves us with CD4+ T lymphocytes and natural killer (NK) cells. CD4+ T lymphocytes are helper T cells that recognize antigens presented on class II major histocompatibility molecules and help activate other immune cells, such as CD8+ T lymphocytes and B lymphocytes. They do not have direct cytotoxic activity against abnormal cells.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Natural killer cells. NK cells are a type of lymphocyte that can recognize and kill abnormal cells, such as virus-infected or cancerous cells, without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation. They do not require recognition of specific antigens presented on major histocompatibility molecules, but instead use a variety of activating and inhibitory receptors to distinguish between normal and abnormal cells. NK cells are important in immune surveillance and can help prevent the development of cancer and viral infections.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "63971e82-e88a-4f73-8777-bd3cd108b0ed",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Natural killer cells.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/11 00:38:49",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-3.5-turbo', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
