                        "cot": "\n\nThe question is asking about a class of immune cells that can lyse abnormal cells without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation. This rules out CD8+ T lymphocytes and CD4+ T lymphocytes, which require activation and priming before they can lyse abnormal cells. It also rules out bone marrow-derived macrophages and band neutrophils, which require opsonization before they can phagocytose and lyse abnormal cells.\n\nThe best answer is therefore D) Natural killer cells, which are able to recognize and lyse abnormal cells without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation. This is because natural killer cells have receptors that can recognize changes in the surface proteins of abnormal cells, such as downregulation of class I major histocompatibility molecules, and can directly lyse these cells.",
