                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nC) Perifascicular and perivascular infiltration on muscle biopsy\n\nThe patient's presentation is consistent with a diagnosis of polymyositis, an autoimmune disorder that causes muscle weakness and inflammation. The elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate and leukocytosis suggest an inflammatory process. The elevated creatine kinase and lactate dehydrogenase levels indicate muscle damage. The muscle biopsy is the gold standard for diagnosing polymyositis, and perifascicular and perivascular infiltration are characteristic findings. Intrafascicular infiltration is more commonly seen in dermatomyositis. Inflammation of synovial spaces on ultrasound is not specific for polymyositis. Positive anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies are seen in myasthenia gravis, which presents with muscle weakness that worsens with activity and improves with rest. A dystrophin gene mutation is seen in Duchenne's muscular dystrophy, which typically presents in childhood and is associated with progressive muscle weakness.",
                        "answers": [
