                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nStep 1: What is the most likely diagnosis?\nStep 2: What is the best test to confirm the diagnosis?\nStep 3: What is the best treatment?\n\nStep 1: Cushing's syndrome\nStep 2: Low dose dexamethasone suppression test\nStep 3: Transsphenoidal surgery\n\nTherefore, the best next step in management is to perform a low dose dexamethasone suppression test.\n\nLow dose dexamethasone suppression test:\n\nDexamethasone suppression test is a test to diagnose Cushing's syndrome. It is performed by giving the patient a dose of dexamethasone (a synthetic glucocorticoid) and measuring the serum cortisol level before and after the administration. In Cushing's syndrome, the pituitary gland is overactive and produces large amounts of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), which in turn stimulates the adrenal glands to produce excess cortisol. Dexamethasone acts to suppress the release of ACTH from the pituitary gland, so if the patient's serum cortisol level does not decrease after taking dexamethasone, then this is a sign that the patient has Cushing's syndrome.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "1247c500-1e24-4dc2-bd88-6cc593ae0e07",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " D.\n\nTranssphenoidal surgery is the best treatment for Cushing's syndrome.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
