                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step. \n\nThe patient has a long erythematous rash covered with multiple clear vesicles and crusty lesions extending from her back to below her right breast.\n\nDifferential diagnosis for a rash:\n- Infectious (viral, bacterial, fungal, parasitic)\n- Allergic\n- Autoimmune\n- Environmental\n- Neoplastic\n\nThe patient has a history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux.\n\nDifferential diagnosis for a rash in a patient with a history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux:\n- Infectious (viral, bacterial, fungal, parasitic)\n- Allergic\n- Autoimmune\n\nThe patient volunteers at an animal shelter three times a week.\n\nDifferential diagnosis for a rash in a patient with a history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux who volunteers at an animal shelter three times a week:\n- Infectious (viral, bacterial, fungal, parasitic)\n- Allergic\n\nThe patient does not smoke or drink alcohol.\n\nDifferential diagnosis for a rash in a patient with a history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux who volunteers at an animal shelter three times a week and does not smoke or drink alcohol:\n- Infectious (viral, bacterial, fungal, parasitic)\n\nThe patient appears healthy and well nourished.\n\nDifferential diagnosis for a rash in a patient with a history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux who volunteers at an animal shelter three times a week and does not smoke or drink alcohol, and appears healthy and well nourished:\n- Infectious (viral, bacterial, fungal, parasitic)\n\nThe patient's temperature is 36.9\u00b0C (98.42\u00b0F), pulse is 76/min, and blood pressure is 145/92 mm Hg.\n\nDifferential diagnosis for a rash in a patient with a history of hypertension and gastroesophageal reflux who volunteers at an animal shelter three times a week and does not smoke or drink alcohol, and appears healthy and well nourished, with a temperature of 36.9\u00b0C (98.42\u00b0F), pulse of