                        "cot_trigger": "lievin-02",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's use step by step inductive reasoning, given the medical nature of the question. \n\nThe first step is to look at the question and identify what information is given, and what is being asked. In this question, we are given information about the patient's condition and his current pain management. We are then asked to identify which medication was most likely recommended by the pain management team. \n\nThe second step is to look at the options and see if any can be eliminated based on the information given in the question. In this question, we are given four options: propofol, ketamine, fentanyl, and midazolam. We know that the recommended agent must be able to reduce the patient's opioid need through interaction with the NMDA-receptor. Based on this information, we can eliminate propofol and midazolam, as they are not known to interact with the NMDA-receptor. \n\nThe third step is to look at the remaining options and see if any can be eliminated based on additional information that can be found in a reliable source. A quick search of \"NMDA-receptor antagonists\" returns ketamine and memantine as the two most common agents. Fentanyl does not appear to interact with the NMDA-receptor, so we can eliminate it as well. This leaves us with ketamine as the most likely recommended agent.",
