                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we know that the patient has metastatic lung cancer and is experiencing severe, unremitting pain. The pain management team has recommended a medication that can reduce his opioid need through interaction with the NMDA-receptor. \n\nOption A, propofol, is a sedative-hypnotic agent and is not typically used for pain management. \n\nOption B, ketamine, is an NMDA-receptor antagonist and is commonly used for pain management, especially in cases of opioid-resistant pain. This is the most likely recommended agent. \n\nOption C, fentanyl, is an opioid and is not an NMDA-receptor antagonist. \n\nOption D, ketorolac, is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) and is not an NMDA-receptor antagonist. \n\nOption E, midazolam, is a benzodiazepine and is not typically used for pain management. \n\nTherefore, the most likely recommended agent is B) Ketamine.",
