                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe question asks for the most likely recommended agent to reduce opioid need through interaction with the NMDA-receptor for a 65-year-old man with metastatic lung cancer experiencing severe, unremitting pain and dose limiting side-effects from oral morphine. \n\nOption A, Propofol, is a sedative-hypnotic agent used for anesthesia induction and maintenance, but it does not interact with the NMDA-receptor and is not typically used for pain management. Therefore, it is unlikely to be the recommended agent.\n\nOption B, Ketamine, is an NMDA-receptor antagonist that can reduce opioid tolerance and dependence, making it a useful adjunct for pain management in patients with cancer. It is also effective for neuropathic pain and can improve mood and quality of life. Therefore, it is a likely recommended agent.\n\nOption C, Fentanyl, is a potent opioid analgesic that does not interact with the NMDA-receptor and is not used to reduce opioid need. Therefore, it is unlikely to be the recommended agent.\n\nOption D, Ketorolac, is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that inhibits prostaglandin synthesis and is used for short-term pain relief. It does not interact with the NMDA-receptor and is not typically used for cancer pain management. Therefore, it is unlikely to be the recommended agent.\n\nOption E, Midazolam, is a benzodiazepine used for sedation and anxiety relief, but it does not interact with the NMDA-receptor and is not typically used for pain management. Therefore, it is unlikely to be the recommended agent.\n\nTherefore, the most likely recommended agent to reduce opioid need through interaction with the NMDA-receptor for a 65-year-old man with metastatic lung cancer experiencing severe, unremitting pain and dose limiting side-effects from oral morphine is Ketamine (Option B).",
                        "answers": [
