                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nD) Maternal diabetes is the strongest predisposing factor for this patient's condition, which is consistent with caudal regression syndrome. Maternal diabetes can lead to fetal malformations, including caudal regression syndrome, due to hyperglycemia-induced damage to the developing fetus. The absence of the sacrum and lumbar vertebrae, small pelvis, shallow intergluteal cleft, and club feet are all characteristic features of caudal regression syndrome. Maternal oligohydramnios, intrauterine alcohol exposure, and chromosomal abnormalities (such as trisomy 13 or 18) can also cause fetal malformations, but they are not as strongly associated with caudal regression syndrome as maternal diabetes.",
