                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has a thyroid nodule that is suspicious for neoplasia based on cytological examination. The planned treatment is near-total thyroidectomy followed by radioiodine therapy. This suggests that the tumor is likely to be a thyroid carcinoma, which can metastasize to other sites in the body.\n\nThe most common initial site of metastasis for thyroid carcinoma is the cervical lymph nodes, which are located near the thyroid gland. Therefore, the correct answer is C) Cervical lymph nodes. \n\nMetastasis to the trachea, hilar lymph nodes, inferior thyroid arteries, or thyrohyoid muscle is less common in thyroid carcinoma. However, metastasis to these sites can occur in advanced cases.",
