                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A LOD (logarithm of the odds) score is a statistical measure used in genetic linkage analysis to determine the likelihood that two loci (locations on a chromosome) are linked, meaning they are inherited together more often than expected by chance. A higher LOD score indicates a higher probability that the loci are linked.\n\nA) LOD Score < 1: This would suggest that the two loci are not likely to be linked, as a LOD score of 1 or greater is generally considered evidence of linkage.\n\nB) LOD Score < 3: This would also suggest that the two loci are not likely to be linked, as a LOD score of 3 or greater is considered strong evidence of linkage.\n\nC) LOD Score < 2: This would again suggest that the two loci are not likely to be linked, as a LOD score of 2 or greater is considered suggestive evidence of linkage.\n\nD) LOD Score > 3: This would suggest that the two loci have a high probability of being closely linked, as a LOD score of 3 or greater is considered strong evidence of linkage.\n\nE) LOD Score = 0: This would suggest that there is no evidence for or against linkage between the two loci.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) LOD Score > 3, as this would most likely suggest that the two loci have a high probability of being closely linked.",
