                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-03",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "we need to determine the patient's acid-base status. The patient has a chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) with a FEV1/FVC ratio of 60%, which indicates airflow limitation. The patient also has a productive cough, fever, and body aches, which suggest an acute exacerbation of COPD. The physical examination reveals diminished breath sounds bilateral, a barrel-shaped chest, and measured breathing through pursed lips, which are all consistent with COPD. The chest X-ray reveals a flattened diaphragm, which is also consistent with COPD.\n\nBased on the patient's history and physical examination, the most likely acid-base disturbance is primary respiratory acidosis. The patient has chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, which causes retention of carbon dioxide (CO2) and leads to respiratory acidosis. The patient's ABG would show an elevated partial pressure of carbon dioxide (PaCO2) and a decreased pH.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Primary respiratory acidosis.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "1e1fd982-873f-4052-8c0a-5d49817f35ce",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
