                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient's presentation is consistent with acute appendicitis, which is most commonly caused by obstruction of the appendiceal lumen by fecaliths or lymphoid hyperplasia. The patient's history of multiple colonic mucosal outpouchings suggests a diagnosis of diverticulosis, which can also cause abdominal pain and constipation. However, acute diverticulitis typically presents with left lower quadrant pain, whereas the patient's pain is in the right lower quadrant. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis in this patient is acute appendicitis.\n\nOf the answer choices, none are directly associated with acute appendicitis or diverticulosis. Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency is a genetic disorder that can cause lung and liver disease. Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disorder is a genetic disorder that affects the kidneys and liver. Ehlers-Danlos syndrome is a group of genetic disorders that affect the connective tissue. Goodpasture syndrome is an autoimmune disorder that affects the lungs and kidneys. Menetrier disease is a rare disorder that causes giant hypertrophic gastritis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is none of the above.",
