                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Identify the disease\nThe patient has a gene defect on chromosome 7, causing dysfunction in a transmembrane protein and leading to recurrent respiratory infections. This description is consistent with cystic fibrosis (CF), a genetic disorder affecting the CFTR protein, which is responsible for regulating the movement of chloride ions across cell membranes.\n\nStep 2: Determine the common pathogens in cystic fibrosis patients\nPatients with CF are prone to respiratory infections due to the thick mucus in their airways. Common pathogens that infect CF patients include:\n\nA) Staphylococcus aureus\nB) Pseudomonas aeruginosa\nC) Burkholderia cepacia\nD) Influenza virus\nE) Candida species (mainly in the context of oral or pulmonary colonization)\n\nStep 3: Identify the most likely pathogen in adulthood\nWhile all of these pathogens can potentially infect a CF patient, the most common and problematic pathogen in adult CF patients is Pseudomonas aeruginosa. This bacterium is known for its ability to form biofilms and develop antibiotic resistance, making it difficult to treat.\n\nAnswer: B) Pseudomonas",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "80ad8435-d484-4eee-be52-eb7e9729f7d2",
