                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " The first thing you notice is sinus tachycardia on the patient's telemetry. This is a heart rate that is too fast. The next thing you notice is that the radial pulse cannot be palpated during inspiration. This is a sign of pericardial tamponade. Pericardial tamponade is a condition in which the pericardium (the sac around the heart) fills with fluid and puts pressure on the heart. This can be a life-threatening condition. The next step in management of this patient should be pericardiocentesis. This is a procedure in which a needle is inserted into the pericardium to remove the fluid.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b0cba28c-373d-45fc-b69a-9a33b0d8f4ce",
