                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nC) Degraded by C1 esterase inhibitor\n\nThe patient's history of meningitis and infection with Streptococcus pneumoniae suggests a deficiency in the complement system, which is an important component of the innate immune system. Specifically, the fact that the patient has a history of meningitis suggests a deficiency in the classical pathway of the complement system, which is activated by antigen-antibody complexes. The classical pathway is initiated by the binding of C1q to antigen-antibody complexes, which then activates a cascade of complement proteins that ultimately lead to the formation of the membrane attack complex (MAC), which creates pores in the cell membrane of the pathogen, leading to its lysis.\n\nHowever, the fact that the patient has a history of infection with Neisseria gonorrhoeae suggests a deficiency in the alternative pathway of the complement system, which is activated by certain bacterial surfaces. Neisseria gonorrhoeae is able to evade the alternative pathway by producing a protein called factor H binding protein, which binds to factor H and prevents it from inactivating C3b, a key component of the alternative pathway.\n\nThe component that is likely deficient in this patient is C1 esterase inhibitor, which is a regulatory protein that inhibits the classical pathway of the complement system. Deficiency in C1 esterase inhibitor leads to uncontrolled activation of the classical pathway, which can result in angioedema (swelling of the skin and mucous membranes) and other symptoms. It is also associated with an increased risk of meningitis and infections with encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae. Therefore, the correct answer is C) Degraded by C1 esterase inhibitor.",
                        "answers": [
