has a history of meningitis and infection with Streptococcus pneumoniae, which are both bacterial infections that can be targeted by MAC.\n- Option E: Induces the alternative complement pathway. This refers to factor D, which is involved in the alternative pathway of complement activation. Deficiency in factor D can lead to increased susceptibility to Neisseria infections, including gonorrhea. Therefore, this is the most likely component that is deficient in the patient.\n\n",
