                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Analyze the patient's symptoms and history\n- Fatigue for several weeks\n- Quit sports due to low energy\n- Decline in academic performance\n- Spending time in the basement playing video games and eating cereal\n- Family history of Hashimoto's thyroiditis and major depressive disorder\n- No history of serious illness\n- No smoking or alcohol consumption\n\nStep 2: Analyze the physical examination findings\n- Conjunctival pallor\n- Inflammation and fissuring of the corners of the mouth\n- Concavity of the fingernails\n\nStep 3: Analyze the laboratory results\n- Hemoglobin: 11.5 g/dL (low)\n- Mean corpuscular volume: 76 \u03bcm3 (low)\n- Platelet count: 290,000/mm3 (normal)\n- Red cell distribution width: 18% (high)\n- Leukocyte count: 7,000/mm3 (normal)\n\nStep 4: Interpret the findings\nThe patient's symptoms, physical examination findings, and laboratory results suggest that he has anemia, specifically microcytic anemia due to the low mean corpuscular volume and high red cell distribution width. The most common cause of microcytic anemia is iron deficiency.\n\nStep 5: Determine the most appropriate initial treatment\nGiven the patient's presentation and laboratory findings, the most appropriate initial treatment would be iron supplementation (Option D). This would help to address the underlying cause of the anemia and improve the patient's symptoms.\n\nIn conclusion, the most appropriate initial step in treatment for this patient is iron supplementation (Option D).",
                        "answers": [
