                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "lievin-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step:\n\n1) The patient has recurrent vomiting that began 5 hours ago.\n\n2) The patient describes the vomitus as bloody and containing black specks.\n\n3) The patient drinks 10\u201312 beers daily and has not seen a physician in 15 years.\n\n4) The patient appears pale.\n\n5) The patient's temperature is 36.7\u00b0C (98.1\u00b0F), pulse is 122/min, and blood pressure is 85/59 mm Hg.\n\n6) Physical examination shows decreased radial pulses, delayed capillary refill, multiple spider angiomata, and a distended abdomen.\n\n7) The patient is oriented to person but not to place or time.\n\nDifferential diagnosis:\n\n1) Gastrointestinal bleeding\n\n2) Peptic ulcer disease\n\n3) Gastritis\n\n4) Esophageal varices\n\n5) Mallory-Weiss tear\n\n6) Gastroenteritis\n\n7) Pancreatitis\n\n8) Hepatic encephalopathy\n\n9) Cirrhosis\n\nThe most likely diagnosis is gastrointestinal bleeding, which can be caused by any of the above conditions. The most likely cause of the patient's current condition is decreased systemic vascular resistance.",
                        "answers": [
