                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's think step by step. This patient has decreased radial pulses, delayed capillary refill, and a distended abdomen. This suggests that he has decreased cardiac output. Cardiac output is determined by stroke volume (the amount of blood ejected per beat) and heart rate. So, this patient likely has decreased cardiac output due to decreased stroke volume.\n\nDecreased stroke volume can be caused by decreased preload (the amount of blood filling the ventricles before they contract), decreased afterload (the resistance to ventricular ejection), or both. In this patient, decreased preload is the most likely cause of decreased stroke volume.\n\nDecreased preload can be caused by decreased systemic vascular resistance (which would lead to increased cardiac output) or decreased cardiac inotropy (which would lead to decreased cardiac output). In this patient, decreased cardiac inotropy is the most likely cause of decreased stroke volume.\n\nCardiac inotropy is determined by the strength of ventricular contraction. This can be affected by the amount of oxygen available to the myocardium, the sympathetic nervous system, and the parasympathetic nervous system. In this patient, the most likely cause of decreased cardiac inotropy is decreased oxygen availability to the myocardium.\n\nThe most likely cause of decreased oxygen availability to the myocardium is decreased fractional tissue oxygen extraction. Fractional tissue oxygen extraction is the amount of oxygen extracted from the blood by the tissues divided by the amount of oxygen in the blood. This patient has multiple risk factors for decreased fractional tissue oxygen extraction, including decreased cardiac output, increased sympathetic nervous system activity, and increased parasympathetic nervous system activity.",
                        "answers": [
