                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the main issue in the patient's presentation.\nThe patient has recurrent vomiting with bloody and black specked vomitus, indicating gastrointestinal bleeding. He also has a history of heavy alcohol use and has not seen a physician in 15 years. He appears pale, has a rapid pulse, low blood pressure, and signs of liver disease (spider angiomata, distended abdomen). He is also confused and disoriented.\n\nStep 2: Determine the physiological response to the patient's condition.\nIn response to the patient's condition, the body will try to compensate to maintain adequate blood flow and oxygen delivery to vital organs. This may involve changes in systemic vascular resistance, tissue oxygen extraction, baroreceptor firing activity, pulmonary vascular resistance, and cardiac inotropy.\n\nStep 3: Compare the patient's response to a healthy adult.\nThe question asks which of the following is most likely decreased in this patient in response to his current condition compared to a healthy adult. This implies that the answer choice should be something that is normally increased in response to stress or illness.\n\nStep 4: Evaluate each answer choice.\nA) Systemic vascular resistance: This is typically increased in response to stress or illness to maintain blood pressure and perfusion to vital organs. However, in this patient, it may be decreased due to vasodilation from alcohol use and liver disease. INCORRECT.\nB) Fractional tissue oxygen extraction: This is the amount of oxygen extracted from the blood by tissues. It may be increased in response to stress or illness to maintain oxygen delivery to vital organs. However, in this patient, it may be decreased due to decreased blood flow and impaired oxygen delivery. CORRECT.\nC) Carotid baroreceptor firing activity: This is the activity of sensory receptors in the carotid arteries that detect changes in blood pressure and help regulate it. It may be increased in response to stress or illness to maintain blood pressure. However, in this patient, it may be decreased due to impaired baroreceptor function from alcohol use and liver disease. INCORRECT.\nD) Pulmonary vascular resistance: This is typically decreased in response to stress or illness to improve oxygenation of the blood. However, in this patient, it may be increased due to hypoxia and pulmonary hypertension from liver disease. INCORRECT.\nE) Cardiac inotropy: This is the strength of contraction of the heart muscle. It may be increased in response to stress or illness to maintain cardiac output. However, in this",
                        "answers": [
