                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "lievin-03",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's differentiate using step by step reasoning like a medical expert: \n\nThe first thing to note is that HgbS is a type of hemoglobin, so it's already present in the patient's red blood cells. The mutation results in the sickling of the red blood cells when exposed to inciting factors such as hypoxic conditions. \n\nNow, hydroxyurea is a medication that is often used to treat sickle cell anemia. It has direct effects on the patient's hemoglobin physiology. \n\nOne direct effect of hydroxyurea is that it decreases the oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin. This is because hydroxyurea inhibits the production of HgbS. \n\nAnother direct effect of hydroxyurea is that it increases levels of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF). This is because hydroxyurea stimulates the production of HgbF. \n\nSo, in summary, the direct effects of hydroxyurea on sickle cell anemia patients are: \n-Decreases the oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin\n-Increases levels of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF)",
                        "answers": [
