                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's think step by step about what happens in sickle cell anemia and how hydroxyurea affects it. \n\nIn sickle cell anemia, there is a mutation in the hemoglobin gene that results in the production of hemoglobin S. This hemoglobin is different from normal hemoglobin in that it is more likely to form long, rod-like structures when exposed to low oxygen levels. These structures can cause the red blood cells to become \"sickled\" and to have difficulty moving through small blood vessels.\n\nHydroxyurea is a medication that is used to treat sickle cell anemia. It works by increasing the levels of fetal hemoglobin in the blood. Fetal hemoglobin is a type of hemoglobin that is typically only found in fetuses and newborns. It is thought to be protective against sickling.\n\nSo, in answer to the question, hydroxyurea has the direct effect of increasing levels of fetal hemoglobin in the blood. This, in turn, may help to protect against sickling.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "Increases levels of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF)",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
