                        "cot": "\nThe patient has a past medical history of asthma, obesity, and a uterine leimyoma which was definitively treated last year. Her last menstrual period was at the age of 47.\nThe patient's vital signs and exam are unremarkable.\nWhich of the following is a potential complication that could occur in this patient?\nThe correct answer is D) Increased bleeding.\n\nThe patient is taking tamoxifen and has regular outpatient follow up appointments to monitor for any recurrence of cancer. Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that is used to treat and prevent breast cancer. It works by blocking the effects of estrogen in the breast tissue, which can slow or stop the growth of cancer cells. Tamoxifen can also reduce the risk of breast cancer in women who are at high risk for the disease.\n\nTamoxifen can cause increased bleeding, so the patient is at risk for this complication. Increased bleeding can occur as a result of tamoxifen's effects on the blood clotting system. Tamoxifen can increase the risk of bleeding by affecting the production of platelets and clotting factors. It can also increase the risk of bleeding by affecting the function of the liver, which is responsible for producing some of the clotting factors.",
