                    {
                        "id": "46b52608-9b80-42a5-ad47-f4e6708ae2d3",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is C) Transmembrane ion channel protein.\n\nThe patient's history of cleft lip and recurrent upper respiratory infections suggests a possible diagnosis of cystic fibrosis (CF), an autosomal recessive disorder caused by mutations in the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene. CFTR is a transmembrane ion channel protein that regulates the transport of chloride and other ions across epithelial cell membranes. Mutations in CFTR lead to abnormal ion transport and thick, sticky mucus in the lungs, pancreas, and other organs.\n\nThe silent cardiac auscultation in the left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line is a classic finding in patients with CF, known as a \"silent chest\" due to decreased air movement in the lungs.\n\nInfertility is a common complication of CF in males due to obstruction of the vas deferens, which carries sperm from the testes to the urethra. This obstruction is caused by thick, sticky mucus in the reproductive tract.\n\nMicrotubule monomeric proteins, lysosomal trafficking regulator proteins, retrograde cytoskeletal motor proteins, and anterograde cytoskeletal motor proteins are not directly related to CF or its pathophysiology.",
                        "answers": [
