                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the question.\n- A 30-year-old male with infertility\n- History of cleft lip and recurrent upper respiratory infections\n- Silent cardiac auscultation in the left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line\n- Possible mutation in a class of proteins\n\nStep 2: Determine the possible classes of proteins that could be affected.\n- Microtubule monomeric protein: involved in cell division and intracellular transport\n- Lysosomal trafficking regulator protein: involved in lysosomal function and intracellular trafficking\n- Transmembrane ion channel protein: involved in ion transport across cell membranes\n- Retrograde cytoskeletal motor protein: involved in intracellular transport from the cell periphery to the nucleus\n- Anterograde cytoskeletal motor protein: involved in intracellular transport from the nucleus to the cell periphery\n\nStep 3: Consider the patient's symptoms and history to narrow down the possible classes of proteins.\n- Infertility: suggests a possible defect in sperm production or transport\n- History of cleft lip and recurrent upper respiratory infections: suggests a possible genetic disorder affecting multiple systems\n- Silent cardiac auscultation: suggests a possible cardiac defect\n\nStep 4: Eliminate unlikely classes of proteins.\n- Microtubule monomeric protein: unlikely to be involved in infertility or cardiac defects\n- Lysosomal trafficking regulator protein: unlikely to be involved in infertility or cardiac defects\n- Transmembrane ion channel protein: unlikely to be involved in infertility or cardiac defects\n- Retrograde cytoskeletal motor protein: unlikely to be involved in cardiac defects\n\nStep 5: Choose the most likely class of proteins based on the patient's symptoms and history.\n- Anterograde cytoskeletal motor protein: mutations in this class of proteins can cause Kartagener syndrome, a genetic disorder that affects multiple systems including the respiratory tract, heart, and reproductive organs. Kartagener syndrome is characterized by situs inversus (reversed organ placement), chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and infertility due to immotile sperm. The silent cardiac auscultation in the left 5th intercostal space at the midclavicular line is consistent with dextrocardia (heart on the right side of the chest) which is a common feature of Kartagener syndrome.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Anterograde cytoskeletal motor protein.",
