                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to note that the patient's history of numbness and weakness in the legs while walking suggests a neurological component to his back pain. Additionally, his past medical history of osteoporosis puts him at increased risk for compression fractures. The fact that his back pain improves with certain activities (riding a bike, pushing a cart) suggests that these activities may be relieving pressure on a nerve or blood vessel. Given his age, smoking history, and hypertension, vascular claudication is also a possibility. \n\nBased on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis is A) Compression fracture. This is supported by the patient's history of worsening back pain after moving furniture, his past medical history of osteoporosis, and the fact that his spine is non-tender with normal mobility in all 4 directions on physical exam. Radiography of the spine will likely confirm the diagnosis. However, further imaging (such as MRI) may be necessary to evaluate for any nerve or vascular involvement.",
                        "answers": [
