                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has gradually worsening back pain that seems to have worsened after moving furniture, which suggests a possible injury or strain. However, the fact that he feels numbness and weakness in his legs while walking suggests a neurological component. The fact that his back pain is improved when he is riding his bike or pushing a cart at the grocery store suggests that movement may be helping to alleviate pressure on a nerve. \n\nGiven the patient's past medical history of osteoporosis, dyslipidemia, and diabetes, he may be at increased risk for spinal compression fractures. However, the fact that his spine is non-tender and has normal mobility in all 4 directions makes this less likely. \n\nA herniated nucleus pulposus (HNP) is a common cause of back pain with neurological symptoms. However, the physical exam findings do not support this diagnosis. \n\nMusculoskeletal strain is a possibility, but the neurological symptoms make this less likely. \n\nSpinal stenosis is a narrowing of the spinal canal that can cause compression of the spinal cord or nerves. This can cause back pain and neurological symptoms, and the fact that the patient's symptoms are relieved with certain movements supports this diagnosis. \n\nVascular claudication is caused by reduced blood flow to the legs, which can cause pain and weakness with walking. However, the fact that the patient's symptoms are specifically related to his back pain makes this less likely. \n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis in this case is spinal stenosis.",
