                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's analyze the patient's symptoms and findings:\n\n1. Altered mental status and confusion\n2. Inability to get out of bed\n3. Lethargy\n4. Hypothermia (rectal temperature of 32\u00b0C)\n5. Bradycardia (pulse of 54/min)\n6. Bradypnea (respirations of 8/min)\n7. Weakness in lower extremities with absent deep tendon reflexes\n8. Dilated pupils poorly reactive to light\n9. Attempting to remove clothing\n\nNow, let's consider each of the drugs listed and their potential to cause these symptoms:\n\nA) Lisinopril: This is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor used to treat hypertension. It is not known to cause the symptoms described in this case.\n\nB) Fluphenazine: This is an antipsychotic medication used to treat schizoaffective disorder. It can cause extrapyramidal symptoms, such as muscle rigidity, tremors, and abnormal movements, but it does not typically cause the symptoms described in this case.\n\nC) Tolbutamide: This is a sulfonylurea used to treat type 2 diabetes mellitus. It can cause hypoglycemia, which could potentially lead to altered mental status and confusion, but it does not typically cause the other symptoms described in this case.\n\nD) Levothyroxine: This is a thyroid hormone replacement medication used to treat hypothyroidism. Overdose or inappropriate dosing can cause symptoms of hyperthyroidism, such as tachycardia, anxiety, and weight loss, but it does not typically cause the symptoms described in this case.\n\nE) Atorvastatin: This is a statin medication used to treat dyslipidemia. It can cause muscle pain and weakness, but it does not typically cause the symptoms described in this case.\n\nBased on the analysis of the drugs and their potential to cause the symptoms described in this case, the most likely cause of these findings is:\n\nB) Fluphenazine",
