                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step: \n\nThe patient has generalized anxiety disorder, which is being treated with citalopram. He comments that his symptoms have improved since initiating the prescribed therapy; however, in the past 2 months, he has been unable to have sexual intercourse due to weak tumescence and low libido.\n\nThe first thing to consider is whether the patient's sexual dysfunction is a side effect of the citalopram. According to the prescribing information for citalopram, sexual side effects are common, occurring in about 1 in 10 patients. These side effects include decreased libido, delayed ejaculation, and anorgasmia. If the patient's sexual dysfunction is a side effect of the citalopram, then the appropriate step would be to lower the dose of citalopram.\n\nIf the patient's sexual dysfunction is not a side effect of the citalopram, then the next step would be to consider other possible causes of the sexual dysfunction, such as low testosterone levels. Low testosterone levels can cause weak tumescence and low libido. The appropriate step in the management of this patient would be to add testosterone replacement therapy.",
