                        "cot": "Let's think step by step. \n\nThe first thing to consider is whether the patient's current medication is effective for his anxiety disorder. If the patient's symptoms have improved since initiating the prescribed therapy, then it is likely that the citalopram is effective and should be continued. \n\nThe next thing to consider is the side effect of citalopram that is causing the patient's sexual dysfunction. If the patient is experiencing weak tumescence and low libido, this is likely due to the citalopram. In this case, the appropriate step would be to lower the dose of citalopram. \n\nIf the patient's anxiety symptoms are not adequately controlled with the current dose of citalopram, then the next step would be to add bupropion. Bupropion is an antidepressant that is often used to treat anxiety disorders. \n\nIf the patient is still experiencing sexual dysfunction after lowering the dose of citalopram, then the next step would be to switch to fluoxetine. Fluoxetine is an antidepressant that is often used to treat anxiety disorders and is less likely to cause sexual side effects than citalopram. \n\nIf the patient is still experiencing sexual dysfunction after switching to fluoxetine, then the next step would be to switch to selegiline. Selegiline is a monoamine oxidase inhibitor that is often used to treat anxiety disorders and is less likely to cause sexual side effects than citalopram or fluoxetine.",
