                        "cot": "the patient's presentation is consistent with ovarian torsion, which occurs when the ovary twists on its vascular pedicle, leading to compromised blood flow and ischemia. The lack of flow in the right ovarian vein on Doppler supports this diagnosis. \n\nThe infundibulopelvic ligament (also known as the suspensory ligament of the ovary) is the most likely ligament to have been involved in this case. This ligament contains the ovarian artery and vein, as well as nerves and lymphatics, and attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall. When the ovary twists, the infundibulopelvic ligament becomes twisted as well, leading to compromised blood flow and ischemia. \n\nA) Broad ligament: This ligament supports the uterus and contains the fallopian tubes, ovaries, and round ligaments. It is not directly involved in ovarian torsion. \n\nB) Round ligament of the uterus: This ligament attaches the uterus to the anterior abdominal wall and is not directly involved in ovarian torsion. \n\nC) Ovarian ligament: This ligament attaches the ovary to the uterus and is not directly involved in ovarian torsion. \n\nD) Cardinal ligament: This ligament supports the cervix and is not directly involved in ovarian torsion. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
