                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is presenting with sudden-onset, severe right flank pain and has a history of recurrent ovarian cysts. The pelvic ultrasound shows a large simple cyst on the right ovary, and there is no flow detected in the right ovarian vein. This suggests that there might be torsion of the ovary, which can lead to compromised blood flow.\n\nNow let's consider the ligaments mentioned in the options:\n\nA) Broad ligament: This is a wide fold of peritoneum that connects the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries to the pelvic sidewalls. It is not directly involved in ovarian torsion.\n\nB) Round ligament of the uterus: This ligament connects the uterus to the labia majora and is not involved in ovarian torsion.\n\nC) Ovarian ligament: This ligament connects the ovary to the uterus. While it is close to the ovary, it is not the primary ligament involved in ovarian torsion.\n\nD) Cardinal ligament: This ligament provides support to the cervix and is not involved in ovarian torsion.\n\nE) Infundibulopelvic ligament: This ligament, also known as the suspensory ligament of the ovary, connects the ovary to the pelvic sidewall and contains the ovarian vessels (artery and vein). In cases of ovarian torsion, this ligament is most likely to be involved as it can twist and compromise blood flow to the ovary.\n\nSo, the correct answer is E) Infundibulopelvic ligament.",
