                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nA) Duodenal peptic ulcer is the most likely diagnosis based on the patient's symptoms of epigastric discomfort and cramping pain that occur mostly after meals and subside several hours after eating. The fact that she avoids eating and has lost weight also supports this diagnosis. The absence of significant findings on physical exam does not rule out this diagnosis. Choledocholithiasis and gallbladder cancer typically present with more severe and persistent pain, and pancreatitis may present with more systemic symptoms such as fever and elevated heart rate. Gastric peptic ulcer may also present with similar symptoms, but the timing of the pain after meals and the fact that the patient avoids eating suggest a duodenal location.",
