                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with epigastric discomfort and cramping pain that occurs mostly after meals and often subsides several hours after eating. This suggests a gastrointestinal issue related to digestion. The fact that she avoids eating and has lost weight also supports this. \n\nThe physical exam is unremarkable, which rules out any acute abdominal issues such as appendicitis or cholecystitis. \n\nThe most likely diagnosis based on the symptoms and history is duodenal peptic ulcer. This is a common condition that causes pain in the upper abdomen, especially after eating. It is often associated with weight loss and can be exacerbated by smoking and alcohol consumption. \n\nCholedocholithiasis and gallbladder cancer can also cause upper abdominal pain, but they are less likely given the patient's symptoms and lack of significant findings on physical exam. \n\nPancreatitis is a possibility, but it typically presents with more severe and persistent pain, as well as other symptoms such as nausea and vomiting. \n\nGastric peptic ulcer is also a possibility, but duodenal ulcers are more common and fit better with the patient's symptoms. \n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis is A) Duodenal peptic ulcer.",
