                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step. \n\nThe first thing to consider is that the infant is tachypneic and has blue-grey discoloration of the lips, fingers, and toes, which suggests cyanosis. \n\nThe next thing to consider is that the infant has increased pulmonary artery pressure, which suggests that there is something wrong with the lungs. \n\nThe most likely cause of the infant's cyanosis is that the lungs are not getting enough oxygen. The most likely cause of this is that the ductus arteriosus is not open, so the oxygen-rich blood from the lungs is not getting to the rest of the body.",
                        "answers": [
