                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "lievin-10",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's think step by step like a medical expert. \n\nThe first thing to consider is the fact that the infant was delivered via C-section. This means that the baby did not have to go through the birth canal, and therefore was not exposed to the stress of labor. \n\nThe next thing to consider is that the baby was meconium-stained, which means that he passed meconium (a black, tarry substance that is the first stool of a newborn) before or during delivery. This can be a sign of fetal distress, and can sometimes lead to respiratory problems. \n\nThe third thing to consider is that the infant has tachypnea (a rapid breathing rate), increased work of breathing, and cyanosis (a blue-grey discoloration of the lips, fingers, and toes). These are all signs of respiratory distress. \n\nThe fourth thing to consider is that the infant has elevated pulmonary artery pressure. This is a sign that the right side of the heart is not pumping blood properly, and is a common cause of respiratory distress in newborns. \n\nThe fifth and final thing to consider is that the infant was started on an inhaled medication that increases smooth muscle cGMP. This medication is used to treat respiratory distress, and the fact that it improved the infant's tachypnea and oxygenation status shows that it is likely the cause of the cyanosis. \n\nIn conclusion, the most likely cause of the infant's cyanosis is the closure of the ductus arteriosus.",
