                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Identify the clinical scenario\n- A newborn boy with tachypnea, increased work of breathing, and elevated pulmonary artery pressure. He initially improved with an inhaled medication that increases smooth muscle cGMP but later developed cyanosis.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the answer choices\nA) Increase in concentration of serum myoglobin - This is not likely to cause cyanosis in this scenario.\nB) Closure of the ductus arteriosus - This is a possibility, as closure of the ductus arteriosus can lead to increased pulmonary artery pressure and cyanosis.\nC) Oxidization of Fe2+ to Fe3+ - This would cause methemoglobinemia, which can lead to cyanosis. However, it is not the most likely cause in this scenario.\nD) Inhibition of mitochondrial cytochrome c oxidase - This would cause a decrease in cellular respiration and oxygen utilization, leading to cyanosis. However, it is not the most likely cause in this scenario.\nE) Allosteric alteration of heme groups - This would affect oxygen binding to hemoglobin, potentially causing cyanosis. However, it is not the most likely cause in this scenario.\n\nStep 3: Choose the most likely cause\n- Based on the clinical scenario and analysis of the answer choices, the most likely cause of this infant's cyanosis is B) Closure of the ductus arteriosus.",
