                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with primary amenorrhea, which means she has not yet had her first menstrual period. The examination findings suggest that she has Mayer-Rokitansky-K\u00fcster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome, which is a congenital disorder characterized by the absence of the uterus and upper part of the vagina.\n\nThe most appropriate treatment for MRKH syndrome is vaginal dilatory therapy, which involves using progressively larger dilators to stretch the vaginal tissue and create a functional vagina. This can improve sexual function and allow for the possibility of future fertility through assisted reproductive technologies.\n\nSpironolactone therapy is used to treat conditions such as hirsutism (excessive hair growth) and acne, but it is not indicated for MRKH syndrome.\n\nGonadectomy (removal of the gonads, which are the ovaries or testes) is not necessary for MRKH syndrome, as the absence of the uterus and upper vagina does not affect the function of the gonads.\n\nTestosterone therapy is not indicated for MRKH syndrome, as it can cause masculinization and virilization in females.\n\nPrednisolone therapy is a corticosteroid medication used to treat inflammation and autoimmune disorders, but it is not indicated for MRKH syndrome.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Vaginal dilatory therapy.",
