                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "1. The patient is a 15-year-old girl who has not had a menstrual period, which suggests a possible issue with her reproductive system.\n2. She has normal height, weight, and BMI, so it's unlikely to be related to malnutrition or other general health issues.\n3. Examination shows secondary breast development and sparse pubic hair, indicating some degree of puberty has occurred.\n4. Abdominal examination reveals bilateral firm, nontender inguinal masses, which could be undescended gonads (testes or ovaries).\n5. Pelvic examination shows a blind-ended vaginal pouch, and ultrasonography does not show a uterus or ovaries, suggesting a congenital abnormality in the development of her reproductive system.\n\nBased on these findings, the most likely diagnosis is Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS) or Swyer Syndrome, both of which can result in the absence of a uterus and ovaries, as well as the presence of undescended gonads.\n\nNow let's consider the treatment options:\n\nA) Spironolactone therapy: This is a diuretic and anti-androgen medication, which would not be appropriate for treating this patient's condition.\n\nB) Gonadectomy: This is the surgical removal of the gonads (testes or ovaries). In the case of AIS or Swyer Syndrome, gonadectomy is often recommended to prevent the risk of gonadal tumors, which can develop in undescended gonads.\n\nC) Testosterone therapy: This would not be appropriate for this patient, as her condition is not related to a lack of testosterone.\n\nD) Vaginal dilatory therapy: This may be considered in some cases to help create a functional vagina, but it would not address the underlying issue of the absent uterus and ovaries.\n\nE) Prednisolone therapy: This is a corticosteroid medication, which would not be appropriate for treating this patient's condition.\n\nBased on the information provided and the likely diagnosis, the most appropriate treatment for this patient's condition is B) Gonadectomy.",
