                        "cot": "Let's think step by step. \n\nThe patient has asthma and is experiencing worsening shortness of breath. She has also had 4 episodes of pneumonia in the last 3 years. These are both red flags that something more serious may be going on. \n\nThe physical exam reveals inspiratory crackles in the right lung base and coarse breath sounds in the bilateral upper lung lobes. This suggests that the patient has consolidation in her lungs, which is often seen in pneumonia. \n\nThe chest radiograph reveals atelectasis in the right lung base. Atelectasis is when part of the lung collapses or doesn't inflate properly. This can be caused by a variety of things, but in this patient, it is likely due to the consolidation seen on physical exam. \n\nThe radiologist also notes \"glove-finger shadows.\" This is a finding seen on chest radiographs in patients with pulmonary edema, which is fluid in the lungs. This is another red flag that something serious is going on. \n\nThe most appropriate therapy in this patient is prednisone. Prednisone is a steroid that is used to treat a variety of conditions, including asthma and pneumonia.",
