                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has a history of asthma, recurrent pneumonia, and elevated IgE levels, which are all suggestive of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). The radiographic findings of atelectasis and glove-finger shadows further support this diagnosis. Therefore, the most appropriate therapy in this patient would be antifungal treatment with fluconazole or itraconazole, along with systemic corticosteroids such as prednisone to reduce inflammation and improve lung function. Isoniazid is used to treat tuberculosis, which is not the likely cause of the patient's symptoms. Caspofungin is not typically used to treat ABPA. Surgical resection of the infiltrates is not necessary in this case and would not address the underlying cause of the patient's symptoms. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Fluconazole and D) Prednisone.",
