                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step. \n\nThe patient has a fever, nausea, and vomiting, which started last night and persisted to this morning. He also has asthma and is currently taking albuterol and fluticasone.\n\nThe differential diagnosis for a patient with these symptoms includes:\n\n-Infectious causes: viral (e.g. influenza, norovirus), bacterial (e.g. food poisoning, pneumonia), fungal (e.g. histoplasmosis)\n\n-Non-infectious causes: gastrointestinal (e.g. gastritis, ulcer), endocrine (e.g. thyroid storm), neoplastic (e.g. leukemia), neurologic (e.g. meningitis)\n\nThe patient's temperature is 102\u00b0F (38.9\u00b0C), blood pressure is 110/60 mmHg, pulse is 95/min, respirations are 17/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air.\n\nThe patient's vital signs are all within normal limits except for the temperature, which is elevated.\n\nOn physical exam, the patient seems uncomfortable. He requests that the lights in the room stay turned off during the physical exam.\n\nThe patient's physical exam is notable for discomfort and light sensitivity.\n\nThe patient's cardiac exam demonstrates a normal rate and rhythm, and his pulmonary exam is clear to auscultation bilaterally.\n\nThe patient's cardiac and pulmonary exams are both normal.\n\nMusculoskeletal exam reveals diminished mobility of the neck in all four directions, with flexion of the neck causing the patient discomfort.\n\nThe patient's musculoskeletal exam is notable for neck stiffness.\n\nLumbar puncture is performed and results are seen below.\n\nCerebrospinal fluid (CSF):\nCell count: 175/mm^3\nCl-: 119 mEq/L\nGlucose: 49 mg/dL\nPressure: 150 mmH2O\nTotal protein: 55 mg/dL\n\nThe patient's CSF results are notable for a high cell count, high chloride, and high protein.\n\nThe most likely diagnosis for this patient is meningitis.\n\nThe best treatment for this patient's most likely diagnosis is IV fluids and monitoring.",
