                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with fever, nausea, vomiting, and neck stiffness, which are all suggestive of meningitis. The lumbar puncture results show an elevated cell count, low glucose, and elevated protein, which are consistent with bacterial meningitis. The patient's past medical history of asthma and current medications are not relevant to the diagnosis or treatment of meningitis.\n\nThe best treatment for bacterial meningitis is prompt administration of antibiotics. Ceftriaxone and vancomycin are the recommended antibiotics for empiric treatment of bacterial meningitis in adults. Therefore, the correct answer is D) Ceftriaxone and vancomycin.\n\nAcyclovir is used to treat viral infections such as herpes simplex virus and varicella-zoster virus, but it is not effective against bacterial meningitis. Amphotericin and 5-flucytosine are used to treat fungal meningitis, which is a rare form of meningitis that typically occurs in immunocompromised patients. Ceftriaxone alone may be effective for treating meningitis caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae, but vancomycin is added to cover for possible infection with methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) or penicillin-resistant Streptococcus pneumoniae. IV fluids and monitoring are important supportive measures, but they do not address the underlying infection.",
