                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 1-month-old girl with persistent vomiting, dehydration, and irritability. She has been projectile vomiting after most feedings for the past week, and now it occurs after every feed. However, her appetite hasn\u2019t decreased, and she seeks milk right after each episode of vomiting. On physical exam, she appears to be mildly dehydrated, listless, and irritable. Her abdomen is soft and nontender with a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region.\n\nThe presence of a palpable olive-shaped mass in the epigastric region suggests pyloric stenosis, a condition in which the muscle at the bottom of the stomach (pylorus) becomes thickened, leading to narrowing of the opening between the stomach and the small intestine. This can cause persistent vomiting, dehydration, and irritability.\n\nIn pyloric stenosis, the loss of gastric contents leads to a loss of hydrogen ions (H+) and chloride ions (Cl-) from the body, resulting in metabolic alkalosis. The loss of hydrogen ions leads to an increase in pH, while the loss of chloride ions leads to a decrease in the concentration of negatively charged ions in the blood, which is compensated for by an increase in bicarbonate ions (HCO3-) to maintain electrical neutrality.\n\nTherefore, the most likely acid-base disturbance associated with this patient\u2019s condition is C) Metabolic alkalosis.",
