                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is D) Type II pneumocytes.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- The patient is a 26-year-old gravida 2 para 1 at 24 weeks gestation who presents with mild abdominal cramps, uterine contractions, and a watery vaginal discharge. She has a history of preterm birth.\n- The vital signs are within normal limits, except for a slightly elevated heart rate (100/min).\n- The pelvic examination reveals cervical softening and shortening, which are signs of cervical effacement and dilation, respectively. These findings are consistent with preterm labor.\n- Transvaginal ultrasound shows a cervical length of 12 mm, which is also consistent with preterm labor. A cervical length of less than 25 mm at 24 weeks gestation is considered a risk factor for preterm birth.\n- A tocolytic (a medication that inhibits uterine contractions) and a single dose of betamethasone (a corticosteroid) are administered. Betamethasone is given to enhance fetal lung maturity and reduce the risk of respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) in preterm infants.\n- Betamethasone stimulates the production of surfactant by fetal type II pneumocytes. Surfactant is a complex mixture of phospholipids and proteins that lines the alveoli of the lungs and reduces surface tension, preventing alveolar collapse and promoting gas exchange. RDS is caused by a deficiency of surfactant, and betamethasone can help prevent it by accelerating the maturation of type II pneumocytes and increasing surfactant production.\n- Therefore, the correct answer is D) Type II pneumocytes.",
