                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nBetamethasone is a corticosteroid that is given to pregnant women at risk of preterm delivery to help mature the fetal lungs. Therefore, the answer must be a cell type that is involved in lung development. \n\nOption A, Type I pneumocytes, are responsible for gas exchange in the lungs and are not directly involved in lung development. \n\nOption B, Goblet cells, produce mucus in the respiratory tract and are not directly involved in lung development. \n\nOption C, Bronchial epithelial cells, line the respiratory tract and are involved in lung development, but they are not the primary target of betamethasone. \n\nOption E, Vascular smooth myocytes, are involved in blood vessel development and are not the primary target of betamethasone. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Type II pneumocytes. Type II pneumocytes produce surfactant, a substance that helps to reduce surface tension in the lungs and prevent lung collapse. Betamethasone stimulates the production of surfactant by these cells, which helps to improve lung function in premature infants.",
