                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe physician is considering treatment with raloxifene for the 65-year-old woman with a T-score of -2.7 SD on bone mineral density screening. Raloxifene is a medication used to treat osteoporosis, but it has potential side effects that need to be considered. The question asks which condition the medication would put her at increased risk of developing.\n\nTo answer this question, we need to review the potential side effects of raloxifene. One of the most significant risks associated with raloxifene is an increased risk of blood clots, including deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and pulmonary embolism (PE). This risk is higher in women who have a history of blood clots or who are immobile for long periods. Therefore, option A, pulmonary embolism, is a possible answer.\n\nCellulitis is a bacterial skin infection that is not related to raloxifene treatment. Therefore, option B can be eliminated.\n\nBreast cancer is a concern for this patient because her mother died of breast cancer at a young age. Raloxifene has been shown to reduce the risk of breast cancer in postmenopausal women, but it can also increase the risk of certain types of breast cancer, such as lobular carcinoma. Therefore, option C is a possible answer.\n\nMandibular osteonecrosis is a rare but serious side effect of some osteoporosis medications, including bisphosphonates. Raloxifene has not been associated with this condition. Therefore, option D can be eliminated.\n\nHypercholesterolemia is not a side effect of raloxifene treatment. Therefore, option E can be eliminated.\n\nIn summary, the medication raloxifene would put the 65-year-old woman at increased risk of developing pulmonary embolism and certain types of breast cancer. Therefore, the correct answer is C) Breast cancer.",
