                        "cot": "it is important to note that raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that is used to prevent and treat osteoporosis in postmenopausal women. While it has some estrogen-like effects on bone, it also has anti-estrogen effects on breast tissue, which can reduce the risk of breast cancer. However, it is also associated with an increased risk of venous thromboembolism (including pulmonary embolism) and should not be used in patients with a history of blood clots or active thromboembolic disease. Therefore, the correct answer is A) Pulmonary embolism. Cellulitis, mandibular osteonecrosis, and hypercholesterolemia are not known side effects of raloxifene. While raloxifene has anti-estrogen effects on breast tissue, it is not completely protective against breast cancer and regular breast cancer screening is still recommended.",
