                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step:\n\nThe patient has a 2-month history of depressed mood and fatigue.\n\n1) The first thing to consider is whether the patient has a medical condition that could be causing her fatigue. Conditions that can cause fatigue include anemia, hypothyroidism, and diabetes.\n\n2) The second thing to consider is whether the patient has a psychiatric condition that could be causing her depressed mood. Conditions that can cause depression include major depressive disorder and bipolar disorder.\n\n3) The third thing to consider is whether the patient's symptoms could be caused by side effects of medications she is taking. Medications that can cause fatigue include beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, and antidepressants. Medications that can cause depression include beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, and antidepressants.\n\nThe patient has a 5-kg (11-lb) weight gain.\n\n1) The first thing to consider is whether the patient has a medical condition that could be causing her weight gain. Conditions that can cause weight gain include Cushing's syndrome and hypothyroidism.\n\n2) The second thing to consider is whether the patient has a psychiatric condition that could be causing her weight gain. Conditions that can cause weight gain include major depressive disorder and binge eating disorder.\n\n3) The third thing to consider is whether the patient's weight gain could be a side effect of a medication she is taking. Medications that can cause weight gain include antipsychotics, antidepressants, and corticosteroids.\n\nThe patient reports trouble concentrating at her job as a preschool teacher and has missed work several times in recent weeks due to generalized fatigue.\n\n1) The first thing to consider is whether the patient has a medical condition that could be causing her trouble concentrating and fatigue. Conditions that can cause trouble concentrating and fatigue include anemia, hypothyroidism, and diabetes.\n\n2) The second thing to consider is whether the patient has a psychiatric condition that could be causing her trouble concentrating and fatigue. Conditions that can cause trouble concentrating and fatigue include major depressive disorder, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, and anxiety disorders.\n\n3) The third thing to consider is whether the patient's symptoms could be caused by side effects of medications she is taking. Medications that can cause trouble concentrating and fatigue include beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, and antidepressants.\n\nThe patient has smoked 2 packs of cigarettes daily for 10 years and is actively trying to quit smoking. A drug is prescribed that will treat the patient's mood disturbance and support smoking cessation. This patient should be counseled about which of the following possible side effects of this drug?\n\n1) The first thing to consider is whether the patient is at risk for any medical conditions that could be exacerbated by the drug she is taking. Conditions that can be exacerbated by the drug include hypertension, arrhythmias, and heart failure.\n\n2) The second thing to consider is whether the patient is at risk for any psychiatric conditions that could be exacerbated by the drug she is taking. Conditions that can be exacerbated by the drug include anxiety disorders and bipolar disorder.\n\n3) The third thing to consider is whether the patient is at risk for any side effects of the drug she is taking. Side effects of the drug include hypotension, urinary retention, tachycardia, and QTc prolongation.",
