                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe 5-year-old boy has a sudden loss of consciousness and low glucose and potassium levels. He has a history of asthma and hospitalizations, and his mother has type 2 diabetes mellitus. A dextrose infusion improves his symptoms, but he has another episode of decreased consciousness overnight. During this episode, his glucose concentration is low, but his insulin concentration is high, and his C-peptide concentration is low. \n\nThe high insulin concentration and low C-peptide concentration suggest that the boy's body is producing insulin but not enough to maintain normal glucose levels. This is consistent with a diagnosis of hypoglycemia due to insulin excess, which can be caused by an insulinoma or exogenous insulin administration. However, the boy has not received exogenous insulin, making an insulinoma the more likely cause. \n\nGlucagonoma, primary adrenal insufficiency, malingering, conversion disorder, and factitious disorder imposed on another are less likely diagnoses because they do not explain the boy's hypoglycemia and high insulin levels. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is B) Glucagonoma.",
