                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe most appropriate procedure for elective termination of pregnancy in this patient is E) Oral mifepristone + misoprostol. Here's why:\n\n- The patient has confirmed pregnancy through a positive urine pregnancy test.\n- She desires to discontinue the pregnancy and has expressed interest in an elective abortion.\n- The patient is in her first trimester of pregnancy (4 weeks since her last menstrual cycle), which is the ideal time for medical abortion.\n- The patient has had two uncomplicated and normal vaginal deliveries at term, indicating that she is a good candidate for medical abortion.\n- The patient and her partner use the withdrawal method for contraception, which suggests that they may prefer a non-invasive method of termination.\n- Oral mifepristone + misoprostol is a safe and effective method of medical abortion that can be used up to 10 weeks of pregnancy. It involves taking mifepristone, which blocks the hormone progesterone needed for the pregnancy to continue, followed by misoprostol, which causes the uterus to contract and expel the pregnancy. This method has a success rate of over 95% and is associated with fewer complications than surgical methods.\n- The other options listed (D&E, oxytocin-induced labor, manual uterine aspiration, and D&C after vacuum evacuation) are surgical methods of abortion that are typically used in later stages of pregnancy or in cases where medical abortion is not feasible or safe. They may also be associated with more complications and require more specialized equipment and training.",
