                        "cot_trigger": "lievin-10",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's think step by step like a medical expert. \n\nThe first thing to consider is the patient's history. This patient has had two uncomplicated vaginal deliveries, so we can rule out any history of complications. \n\nThe next thing to consider is the patient's current condition. She is 42 weeks pregnant, so we know that the baby is full-term. She is also 165 cm (5 ft 5 in) tall and weighs 86 kg (200 lb); BMI is 33 kg/m2. Her temperature is 36.8\u00b0C (98.4\u00b0F), pulse is 90/min, respirations are 14/min, and blood pressure is 110/80 mmHg. Examination shows a nontender, soft uterus consistent in size with a 42-weeks' gestation. \n\nBased on the information given, we can rule out any complications with the mother's health. \n\nThat leaves us with the child's health. The child is at greatest risk for polyhydramnios, acute respiratory distress syndrome, meconium aspiration, and placental abruption. \n\nPolyhydramnios is a condition in which there is too much amniotic fluid surrounding the baby. This can cause the baby to have difficulty breathing and can lead to premature labor. \n\nAcute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) is a condition in which the lungs are unable to provide enough oxygen to the body. This can be caused by a number of things, including meconium aspiration. \n\nMeconium aspiration is a condition in which the baby inhales meconium (the baby's first stool) into the lungs. This can cause the lungs to become inflamed and can lead to ARDS. \n\nPlacental abruption is a condition in which the placenta separates from the uterus before the baby is born. This can cause the baby to be born prematurely and can lead to a number of complications, including meconium aspiration.",
