                        "cot": "\n\nD) Meconium aspiration\n\nThis patient is at increased risk for post-term pregnancy complications, including meconium aspiration syndrome. Meconium aspiration occurs when a fetus passes stool (meconium) into the amniotic fluid, which can then be aspirated into the lungs during delivery. This can cause respiratory distress and other complications in the newborn. Other potential complications of post-term pregnancy include macrosomia, fetal distress, and placental insufficiency. Polyhydramnios, caudal regression syndrome, and placental abruption are not necessarily more common in post-term pregnancies. Acute respiratory distress syndrome is a complication that can occur in the postpartum period, but is not directly related to post-term pregnancy.",
