                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the question.\n- 36-year-old woman, gravida 3, para 2\n- 42 weeks' gestation\n- Uncomplicated pregnancy\n- Two previous uncomplicated vaginal deliveries at 41 and 42 weeks' gestation\n- BMI of 33 kg/m2\n- Nontender, soft uterus consistent in size with a 42-weeks' gestation\n\nStep 2: Analyze the options.\nA) Polyhydramnios: Excessive amniotic fluid can occur in post-term pregnancies, but it is not the most common or severe complication.\nB) Acute respiratory distress syndrome: This is a severe lung condition that can occur in premature babies, but it is not directly related to post-term pregnancies.\nC) Caudal regression syndrome: This is a rare congenital disorder that affects the development of the lower spine and legs. It is not directly related to post-term pregnancies.\nD) Meconium aspiration: This is a serious complication that can occur when a baby inhales meconium (the first stool) during delivery. It is more common in post-term pregnancies.\nE) Placental abruption: This is a serious complication that can occur when the placenta separates from the uterus before delivery. It is more common in post-term pregnancies.\n\nStep 3: Choose the best answer.\nBased on the information provided, the most likely complication for this patient's child is meconium aspiration (option D). This is because post-term pregnancies are associated with an increased risk of meconium-stained amniotic fluid, which can lead to meconium aspiration syndrome. The other options are less likely or not directly related to post-term pregnancies.",
