                        "cot": "A) Polyhydramnios - This is a condition where there is an excessive amount of amniotic fluid in the uterus. There is no information in the question stem to suggest that this patient has polyhydramnios.\n\nB) Acute respiratory distress syndrome - This is a severe lung condition that can occur in newborns, but there is no information in the question stem to suggest that this patient's child is at risk for this complication.\n\nC) Caudal regression syndrome - This is a rare birth defect that affects the lower spine and can cause various other abnormalities. There is no information in the question stem to suggest that this patient's child is at risk for this complication.\n\nD) Meconium aspiration - This is a condition where a newborn inhales a mixture of meconium (the first stool) and amniotic fluid during labor or delivery. This can cause respiratory problems and is more likely to occur in pregnancies that go beyond 40 weeks' gestation. Since this patient is at 42 weeks' gestation, her child is at a greater risk for meconium aspiration.\n\nE) Placental abruption - This is a serious condition where the placenta separates from the uterus before the baby is born. There is no information in the question stem to suggest that this patient is at risk for placental abruption.\n\nBased on the information provided, the correct answer is D) Meconium aspiration.",
