                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's derive the differential diagnosis step by step: \n\n1) The patient has a third-degree laceration in the vagina extending into the perineum at the 6 o'clock position. This suggests that the laceration involves the anal sphincter complex. \n2) The patient has a history of gestational diabetes. This increases the risk of macrosomia, which increases the risk of shoulder dystocia and associated complications like brachial plexus injury. \n3) The patient has a history of vacuum-assisted delivery. This increases the risk of perineal lacerations. \n\nBased on the above information, the most likely complications are: \n\nA) Urge incontinence: This is a type of incontinence that is characterized by a strong, sudden urge to urinate followed by an involuntary loss of urine. It is caused by damage to the nerves that control the bladder. \nB) Vesicovaginal fistula: This is a fistula (an abnormal connection) between the bladder and the vagina. It is caused by damage to the tissues that separate the bladder and the vagina. \nC) Fecal incontinence: This is a condition in which a person cannot control their bowel movements. It is caused by damage to the muscles and nerves that control the bowel. \nD) Stress incontinence: This is a type of incontinence that is characterized by leakage of urine with activities that put stress on the bladder, such as coughing, sneezing, or laughing. It is caused by damage to the muscles and tissues that support the bladder.",
                        "answers": [
