                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with several episodes of depressed mood over the past 3 years, which do not coincide with any specific life events and resolve after 4-6 weeks. She also reports occasional episodes of elevated mood over the past ten years, during which she is very productive at work and requires less sleep than usual. Her medical history is notable for obesity, type I diabetes mellitus, and rheumatoid arthritis, and she smokes marijuana occasionally. On exam, she has a flattened affect but is cooperative and answers questions thoroughly.\n\nThe most likely diagnosis in this patient is Bipolar II disorder. This is because she has experienced episodes of both depression and hypomania (elevated mood) over the past ten years, which is a hallmark of Bipolar II disorder. Additionally, her symptoms of depression (depressed mood, sleep disturbances, and poor appetite) are consistent with a depressive episode. The fact that her episodes of elevated mood do not meet the criteria for a manic episode (as they are not severe enough to cause marked impairment in social or occupational functioning, and do not include psychotic symptoms) is also consistent with a diagnosis of Bipolar II disorder.\n\nCyclothymic disorder is less likely, as this diagnosis requires the presence of numerous periods of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that do not meet the criteria for a full manic or depressive episode, and must persist for at least 2 years in adults (1 year in children and adolescents).\n\nPersistent depressive disorder (formerly known as dysthymia) is also less likely, as this diagnosis requires the presence of a depressed mood that persists for at least 2 years, with symptoms that are not severe enough to meet the criteria for a major depressive episode.\n\nSchizoaffective disorder is less likely, as this diagnosis requires the presence of both psychotic symptoms (such as delusions or hallucinations) and mood symptoms (such as depression or mania) that occur together and are not better explained by another diagnosis.\n\nBipolar I disorder is less likely, as this diagnosis requires the presence of at least one manic episode, which is not described in this patient.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "07d14fc1-cd37-4350-9c29-946412385edd",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Bipolar II disorder.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
