                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-09",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient reports experiencing both depressive and elevated mood episodes, which suggests a mood disorder. However, the elevated mood episodes do not meet the criteria for a manic episode, as there is no mention of significant impairment or psychotic symptoms. This rules out Bipolar I disorder. \n\nBipolar II disorder is characterized by recurrent episodes of major depression and hypomania, which is a less severe form of mania. The patient's elevated mood episodes may fit the criteria for hypomania, but more information is needed to confirm this diagnosis. \n\nCyclothymic disorder is a milder form of bipolar disorder, characterized by numerous periods of hypomanic and depressive symptoms that do not meet the criteria for a full-blown episode. This diagnosis may be considered, but again, more information is needed to confirm it. \n\nPersistent depressive disorder (formerly known as dysthymia) is a chronic form of depression that lasts for at least 2 years. While the patient reports recurrent episodes of depression, they do not meet the criteria for persistent depressive disorder, as they resolve after 4-6 weeks. \n\nSchizoaffective disorder is a mood disorder that also involves psychotic symptoms, such as hallucinations or delusions. There is no mention of psychotic symptoms in this patient, so this diagnosis is unlikely. \n\nBased on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis in this patient is Bipolar II disorder. However, further evaluation and assessment is needed to confirm this diagnosis and rule out other possibilities.",
