                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key symptoms\n- Social isolation and loneliness\n- Extreme sensitivity to criticism\n- Low self-esteem and negative self-image\n- Avoidance of social situations and extracurricular activities\n- Fear of rejection\n\nStep 2: Match the symptoms to the diagnostic criteria for personality disorders\n- Schizotypal personality disorder: characterized by odd beliefs, magical thinking, and eccentric behavior, but not necessarily social isolation or fear of rejection\n- Generalized anxiety disorder: characterized by excessive worry and anxiety about a variety of situations, but not necessarily social isolation or fear of rejection\n- Avoidant personality disorder: characterized by social inhibition, feelings of inadequacy, and fear of rejection, which often lead to social isolation and avoidance of activities that involve interpersonal contact\n- Paranoid personality disorder: characterized by distrust and suspicion of others, but not necessarily social isolation or fear of rejection\n- Dependent personality disorder: characterized by a pervasive need to be taken care of, which may lead to social isolation if the person feels they cannot function without others\n\nStep 3: Choose the most likely diagnosis\nBased on the symptoms described, the most likely diagnosis is avoidant personality disorder. The daughter's fear of rejection and negative self-image are consistent with this diagnosis, as is her avoidance of social situations and extracurricular activities. The extreme sensitivity to criticism may also be a feature of this disorder. Schizotypal personality disorder and paranoid personality disorder are less likely because they do not fit the pattern of social isolation and fear of rejection. Generalized anxiety disorder and dependent personality disorder may also involve social isolation, but they do not fit the full range of symptoms described.",
