                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus is the most likely cause of this patient's findings. The symptoms of painful double vision that occur only when looking sideways, along with the adduction deficits and nystagmus, suggest dysfunction of the medial rectus subnucleus and medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) in the brainstem. Bilateral disc hyperemia may be a sign of optic neuritis, which can be associated with demyelination. The patient's history of myopia and recent head trauma are not directly related to her current symptoms. Compressive tumor of the pons, subdural hematoma extending into the orbit, and infarction of the brainstem would be less likely to cause the specific pattern of eye movement abnormalities seen in this patient. Thiamine deficiency can cause a variety of neurological symptoms, but is not a likely cause of this patient's findings.",
