                    {
                        "id": "f52195a4-f46d-4709-bf0b-a3e2f984229d",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has periodic painful double vision that occurs only when looking sideways, indicating a problem with lateral gaze. She also has an adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye when looking in the opposite direction, indicating an internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO). \n\nBilateral disc hyperemia suggests increased intracranial pressure. \n\nThe most likely cause of these findings is demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF), which connects the oculomotor nucleus in the midbrain to the abducens nucleus in the pons. Damage to the MLF disrupts the coordination of eye movements during lateral gaze, leading to INO. \n\nCompressive tumor of the pons can also cause INO, but it is less likely to present with periodic painful double vision. \n\nOxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency can cause Wernicke's encephalopathy, which can present with ophthalmoplegia, but it is not consistent with the patient's history and symptoms. \n\nSubdural hematoma extending into the orbit is unlikely to cause INO and bilateral disc hyperemia. \n\nInfarction of the brainstem can cause ophthalmoplegia, but it is less likely to present with INO and bilateral disc hyperemia. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus.",
                        "answers": [
