                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key findings in the patient's presentation.\n- Periodic painful double vision when looking sideways\n- Adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye when looking in the opposite direction\n- Bilateral disc hyperemia\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible causes of these findings.\n- Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus: This can cause internuclear ophthalmoplegia, which is characterized by an adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye when looking in the opposite direction. However, disc hyperemia is not typically seen in this condition.\n- Compressive tumor of the pons: This can cause a variety of eye movement abnormalities, including internuclear ophthalmoplegia, but disc hyperemia is not typically seen.\n- Oxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency: Thiamine deficiency can cause optic neuropathy, which can present with disc hyperemia. However, it is unlikely to cause the specific eye movement abnormalities seen in this patient.\n- Subdural hematoma extending into the orbit: This is a rare condition that can cause proptosis (bulging of the eye), but it is not typically associated with the specific eye movement abnormalities seen in this patient.\n- Infarction of the brainstem: This can cause a variety of eye movement abnormalities, including internuclear ophthalmoplegia, and disc hyperemia can be seen in some cases.\n\nStep 3: Choose the most likely cause based on the findings and possible causes.\n- Based on the patient's presentation, the most likely cause of her findings is infarction of the brainstem. This can cause internuclear ophthalmoplegia and disc hyperemia, and it is consistent with the patient's history of a recent fall and normal CT scan at that time.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Infarction of the brainstem.",
                        "answers": [
