                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 25-year-old woman with periodic painful double vision for 3 days, occurring only when looking sideways. She has myopia and has been wearing corrective lenses for 10 years. Ten days ago, she fell off her bike and had a CT scan of the head, which showed no abnormalities. She is on an oral contraceptive and has bilateral disc hyperemia on fundoscopy. Her best corrected visual acuity is 20/40 in each eye. She has an adduction deficit in the right eye and nystagmus in the left eye when looking left; she has an adduction deficit in the left eye and nystagmus in the right eye when looking right.\n\nThe most likely cause of this patient's findings is demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF). The MLF is a pathway in the brainstem that connects the oculomotor nuclei and the abducens nuclei, which are responsible for eye movements. Demyelination of the MLF can cause internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO), which is characterized by an adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye when looking in the direction of the affected eye. This is consistent with the patient's symptoms of adduction deficit and nystagmus in both eyes when looking in opposite directions.\n\nCompressive tumor of the pons is less likely because the CT scan of the head showed no abnormalities. Oxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency is also less likely because the patient's symptoms are not consistent with Wernicke's encephalopathy, which is associated with thiamine deficiency. Subdural hematoma extending into the orbit is unlikely because the patient's symptoms are not consistent with orbital involvement. Infarction of the brainstem is also less likely because the patient's symptoms are not consistent with a specific vascular territory. \n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's findings is demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "32ea29ce-4668-421a-aa08-547be2ae5cca",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
