                        "instruction": "qa-01",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 78-year-old right-handed man who suddenly experienced speech changes while chatting with his daughter. His speech became distorted with random and disconnected words that made no sense, and he seems physically frustrated that no one understands him. Additionally, physical examination demonstrates weakness of the right arm. \n\nBased on these symptoms, we can infer that the patient is likely experiencing a stroke, specifically a left hemisphere stroke, as the left hemisphere of the brain is responsible for language function in most individuals. \n\nTo determine the location of the damage, we can consider the symptoms. The patient's speech changes suggest damage to the language centers of the brain, which are primarily located in the left hemisphere. The weakness of the right arm suggests damage to the motor cortex, which is also located in the left hemisphere. \n\nOption A, damage to the left frontal lobe, is a possibility as this area is involved in language production and motor function. However, it is not the best explanation for the patient's symptoms as damage to this area alone would not explain the weakness of the right arm. \n\nOption B, damage to the left internal capsule, is a more likely explanation as this area contains the fibers that connect the motor cortex to the spinal cord and brainstem. Damage to this area can result in weakness or paralysis on the opposite side of the body, which would explain the patient's weakness of the right arm. Additionally, the internal capsule contains fibers that connect the language centers of the brain, so damage to this area could also explain the patient's speech changes. \n\nOption C, damage to the left temporal lobe, is less likely as this area is primarily involved in auditory processing and memory function, rather than language production or motor function. \n\nOption D, damage to the right frontal lobe, is unlikely as this area is not typically involved in language production or motor function. \n\nOption E, damage to the right temporal lobe, is also unlikely as this area is primarily involved in visual processing and memory function, rather than language production or motor function. \n\nTherefore, the best explanation for the patient's symptoms is damage to the left internal capsule, making option B the correct answer.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "5aff7d96-3a3d-4e78-b4a8-3b6b9ffcea28",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Left internal capsule.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
