                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key features of the patient's presentation.\n- 6-year-old Russian boy who recently immigrated to the United States\n- Fever and dyspnea\n- Grayish-white pseudomembrane and uneven elevation of the soft palate\n- Marked enlargement of the cervical lymph nodes\n\nStep 2: Recall the possible organisms that can cause these symptoms.\n- Yeast with pseudohyphae: typically causes oral thrush, but not associated with marked cervical lymph node enlargement\n- Gram-positive bacteria producing exotoxin that acts via ADP ribosylation: examples include Corynebacterium diphtheriae (causes diphtheria) and Clostridium difficile (causes pseudomembranous colitis), both of which can cause pseudomembrane formation, but only C. diphtheriae is associated with marked cervical lymph node enlargement\n- Gram-positive cocci with hyaluronic acid capsule: examples include Streptococcus pyogenes (causes pharyngitis and tonsillitis), but not associated with pseudomembrane formation\n- Gram-negative encapsulated bacteria producing IgA protease: examples include Haemophilus influenzae (causes meningitis, epiglottitis, and pneumonia), but not associated with pseudomembrane formation\n- Gram-negative bacteria producing exotoxin that inactivates elongation factor 2: example is Corynebacterium pseudotuberculosis (causes caseous lymphadenitis), but not commonly associated with pseudomembrane formation\n\nStep 3: Choose the best answer.\nBased on the key features of the patient's presentation and the possible organisms that can cause these symptoms, the most likely answer is B) Gram-positive bacteria producing exotoxin that acts via ADP ribosylation, specifically Corynebacterium diphtheriae causing diphtheria. This organism is known to cause pseudomembrane formation in the oropharynx and marked cervical lymph node enlargement.",
