                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nB) Has a rabbit reservoir\n\nThe patient's history of interacting with local animals, combined with the presence of patchy infiltrates on chest radiograph, suggests that the most likely organism responsible for his symptoms is Francisella tularensis, which is associated with a rabbit reservoir. Other symptoms, such as fever, headache, and diarrhea, are also consistent with tularemia. Endospore-forming organisms are typically associated with Bacillus and Clostridium species, which are not likely to be responsible for this patient's symptoms. The absence of a cell wall is a characteristic of Mycoplasma species, which can cause pneumonia, but this organism is not associated with a rabbit reservoir. Psittacosis, which is associated with parrot owners, can cause pneumonia, but it is not typically associated with diarrhea or hyponatremia. Finally, Legionella pneumophila, which can be transmitted by air conditioning, can also cause pneumonia, but it is not associated with a rabbit reservoir.",
