                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe patient has a history of alcoholism.\nThe patient has flank pain and decreased urination.\nThe urine microscopy demonstrated abundant square crystals of calcium oxalate that looked like folded envelopes.\nThe answer is C, Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis.\n\nCalcium oxalate stones are a common cause of flank pain and decreased urination. The most common cause of metabolic acidosis is increased production of hydrogen ions or increased loss of bicarbonate ions. The patient has a history of alcoholism, which can lead to a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis.\n\nThe metabolic acidosis can be either high-output or low-output. A high-output low-input state can be caused by diarrhea, vomiting, or hyperventilation. A low-output high-input state can be caused by renal failure, ingestion of diuretics, or excessive ingestion of salt.\n\nIn this patient, the most likely cause of metabolic acidosis is the ingestion of large amounts of alcohol, which causes a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis.\n\nThe answer is C, Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis.",
