                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Respiratory alkalosis is most likely to be seen in this patient.\nThe correct answer is Respiratory alkalosis.\n\nThe patient has a history of alcoholism and was found lying unconscious on a bench at a local park. These findings are consistent with acute ethanol intoxication. Acute ethanol intoxication is associated with respiratory alkalosis due to the accumulation of acetaldehyde, an intermediate metabolite of ethanol.\n\nRespiratory alkalosis is characterized by an increased pH, decreased pCO2, and increased pO2. This is due to the hyperventilation that occurs in response to the accumulation of acetaldehyde.\n\nThe other answer choices can be ruled out based on the patient's history and physical examination.\n\nMetabolic alkalosis is characterized by an increased pH, increased pCO2, and decreased pO2. This is due to the accumulation of hydrogen ions.\n\nAnion gap metabolic acidosis is characterized by an increased anion gap, decreased pH, and increased pCO2. This is due to the accumulation of unmeasured anions, such as lactate.\n\nNon-anion gap metabolic acidosis is characterized by an increased anion gap, decreased pH, and increased pCO2. This is due to the accumulation of unmeasured anions, such as lactate.\n\nRespiratory acidosis is characterized by an increased pCO2, decreased pH, and increased pO2. This is due to the accumulation of unmeasured anions, such as lactate.\n\nRespiratory alkalosis is characterized by an increased pH, decreased pCO2, and increased pO2. This is due to the hyperventilation that occurs in response to the accumulation of acetaldehyde.",
