                        "cot": "\n\nThe disease in question is likely cystic fibrosis, which is an autosomal recessive disorder. The female patient is a carrier of the disease, as she has one copy of the mutated gene and one normal copy. Her husband is not a carrier, as the prevalence of the disease in his population is only 1/100.\n\nTo determine the chance that their child will have the disease, we need to use a Punnett square to calculate the possible genotypes of their offspring:\n\n|   | C | c |\n|---|---|---|\n| C | CC (no disease) | Cc (carrier) |\n| c | Cc (carrier) | cc (disease) |\n\nThere are four possible outcomes, with one possibility (cc) resulting in the child having the disease. The chance of this occurring is 1/4, or 25%.\n\nHowever, we need to take into account that the female patient is a carrier of the disease, which means that there is a 50% chance that she will pass on the mutated gene to her offspring. This means that the chance of their child having the disease is actually 1/2 x 1/4, or 1/8.\n\nTo convert this to a percentage, we multiply by 100, giving us 12.5%. Therefore, the answer is closest to option A) 9/100.",
