                        "cot": "\n\nThe disease in question is likely cystic fibrosis, which is an autosomal recessive disorder. This means that an individual must inherit two copies of the mutated gene (one from each parent) to develop the disease. \n\nThe female patient in this scenario has the disease phenotype, which means she must have inherited two copies of the mutated gene. Her husband does not have the disease phenotype, but we do not know his genotype (whether he is a carrier or not). \n\nWe are given that the prevalence of the disease in the husband's population is 1/100. This means that the frequency of carriers in the population is q = 0.1 (since the disease is rare, we can assume that the population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium and use the equation p + q = 1, where p is the frequency of the normal allele). \n\nWe can use a Punnett square to determine the probability of their child inheriting two copies of the mutated gene:\n\n|   | Normal allele (p) | Mutated allele (q) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mutated allele (q) | qq (disease phenotype) | qq (disease phenotype) |\n| Normal allele (p) | pq (carrier) | pq (carrier) |\n\nEach parent contributes one allele to their child, so the probability of their child inheriting a mutated allele from the mother is 1 (since she has two copies of the mutated allele). The probability of their child inheriting a mutated allele from the father is q (since we don't know his genotype, we assume he has a 50% chance of passing on the mutated allele if he is a carrier). \n\nTherefore, the probability of their child inheriting two copies of the mutated allele (and having the disease phenotype) is:\n\n1 x q = 0.1\n\nSo the answer is A) 9/100.",
