                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key findings in the patient's history and physical examination.\n\n- 7-day-old newborn boy\n- Fever, irritability, and generalized erythema\n- Tried to control symptoms with acetaminophen\n- Blisters around buttocks, hands, and feet\n- Vital signs: body temperature 39.0\u00b0C (102.3\u00b0F), heart rate 130/min, respiratory rate 45/min\n- Ears, nose, and oral mucosa preserved their integrity\n- Skin presents with diffuse blanching erythema and flaccid blisters with a positive Nikolsky\u2019s sign\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible diagnoses based on the key findings.\n\n- Scarlet fever: Unlikely, as there is no mention of a sore throat or rash on the face, neck, and chest.\n- Scalded skin syndrome: Possible, as there are blisters and a positive Nikolsky\u2019s sign, but this condition is usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus and is more common in older children.\n- Impetigo: Unlikely, as this condition usually presents with honey-colored crusts and is not associated with fever or blisters.\n- Erysipela: Unlikely, as this condition usually affects adults and presents with a well-demarcated, raised, and painful rash.\n- Pyoderma: Possible, as this is a general term for skin infections caused by bacteria, but more information is needed to make a specific diagnosis.\n\nStep 3: Choose the most likely diagnosis based on the key findings.\n\nThe most likely diagnosis in this patient is Scalded skin syndrome, as it presents with blisters and a positive Nikolsky\u2019s sign, and is often caused by Staphylococcus aureus. However, it is important to consider other possible causes and perform appropriate diagnostic tests (e.g. blood cultures, skin biopsy) to confirm the diagnosis and guide treatment.",
