                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 7-day-old newborn with fever, irritability, generalized erythema, and blisters around the buttocks, hands, and feet. The skin presents with diffuse blanching erythema and flaccid blisters with a positive Nikolsky\u2019s sign. \n\nA) Scarlet fever - This is a streptococcal infection that presents with a characteristic rash, but it typically starts with a sore throat and does not cause blisters or a positive Nikolsky's sign.\n\nB) Scalded skin syndrome - This is caused by Staphylococcus aureus exfoliative toxins and presents with widespread erythema, flaccid blisters, and a positive Nikolsky's sign. This is a likely diagnosis for this patient.\n\nC) Impetigo - This is a superficial skin infection caused by Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes. It presents with honey-colored crusts and pustules but does not cause widespread erythema or a positive Nikolsky's sign.\n\nD) Erysipela - This is a superficial skin infection caused by Streptococcus pyogenes. It presents with well-demarcated, raised, and erythematous plaques but does not cause blisters or a positive Nikolsky's sign.\n\nE) Pyoderma - This is a general term for a purulent skin infection, which can be caused by various bacteria. It does not specifically describe the clinical presentation seen in this patient.\n\nBased on the clinical presentation, the most likely diagnosis in this patient is Scalded skin syndrome (B).",
