                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 60-year-old woman with severe vulvar itching that has been gradually worsening for the past year. She has tried over-the-counter lubricants, but they do not seem to provide substantial relief. Her medical history is remarkable for diet-controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide. Menopause occurred at 52 years of age. She has been sexually active with her husband until recently and reports severe pain during vaginal intercourse. The physical examination reveals dry, thin, white plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area with atrophy of the labia minora. The clitoris appears retracted. The perianal skin appears pale and wrinkled.\n\nThe most likely diagnosis is lichen sclerosus. Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory skin condition that affects the genital and perianal areas. It is more common in postmenopausal women and is associated with autoimmune disorders, such as type 1 diabetes mellitus and thyroid disease. The symptoms of lichen sclerosus include severe itching, pain during intercourse, and white, thin, plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area. The clitoris may appear retracted, and the labia minora may be atrophied. The perianal skin may also be affected.\n\nSquamous cell hyperplasia is a benign condition that causes thickening of the skin. It is not typically associated with severe itching or pain during intercourse.\n\nSquamous cell carcinoma of the vulva is a rare but serious condition that can cause itching and pain. However, it typically presents as a lump or sore on the vulva, rather than white, thin, plaque-like lesions.\n\nLichen planus is another chronic inflammatory skin condition that can affect the genital area. However, it typically presents as purple, itchy, flat-topped bumps, rather than white, thin, plaque-like lesions.\n\nAtrophic vaginitis is a condition that occurs when the vaginal walls become thin and dry due to decreased estrogen levels. It can cause itching and pain during intercourse, but it does not typically cause white, thin, plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area.\n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis for this patient is lichen sclerosus.",
