                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Let's think step by step about what happens in the glomerular capillaries. \n\nFluid enters the capillaries from the glomerular filtrate. This fluid has a low oncotic pressure because it has been filtered from the blood. \n\nThe fluid leaves the capillaries and enters the Bowman's capsule. The oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries is therefore less than the oncotic pressure of the fluid entering the capillaries. \n\nThe hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is also less than the hematocrit of the fluid entering the capillaries. This is because some of the fluid has been filtered out of the blood and into the Bowman's capsule. \n\nThe glucose concentration in the capillaries is the same as the glucose concentration in the glomerular filtrate. This is because the glomerular filtrate is a filtrate of the blood, and so it contains the same concentration of glucose as the blood does. \n\nConstriction of the afferent arteriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulus. This is because the afferent arteriole is the artery that supplies blood to the glomerulus.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "Constriction of afferent aeriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulas",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
