                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " \n\nFirst, oncotic pressure is the pressure created by the presence of large molecules in a solution. So the oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries would be greater than the oncotic pressure of the fluid entering it. \n\nSecond, the glucose concentration in the capillaries is not the same as the glucose concentration in the glomerular filtrate. The glomerular filtrate has a higher concentration of glucose because it has not been filtered by the kidneys yet. \n\nThird, constriction of the afferent arteriole would actually increase the blood flow to the glomerulus. This is because constriction of the afferent arteriole decreases the resistance to blood flow, which in turn increases the blood flow. \n\nFourth, the hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is greater than the hematocrit of the fluid entering it. This is because the fluid that enters the capillaries has been filtered by the kidneys, so it has a lower concentration of red blood cells.",
