                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-09",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nA) The oncotic pressure of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of fluid entering it - This is true. The oncotic pressure of the glomerular filtrate is lower than that of the blood in the capillaries, which allows for filtration to occur.\n\nB) Glucose concentration in the capillaries is the same as that in glomerular filtrate - This is true. Glucose is a small molecule that is freely filtered by the glomerulus, so its concentration in the filtrate is the same as in the blood.\n\nC) Constriction of afferent arteriole decreases the blood flow to the glomerulus - This is true. Constriction of the afferent arteriole reduces the amount of blood flowing into the glomerulus, which decreases the glomerular filtration rate.\n\nD) Hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of the fluid entering it - This is not true. Hematocrit refers to the percentage of red blood cells in the blood, and it does not change during filtration. Therefore, the hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is the same as that of the fluid entering it. \n\nTherefore, the answer is D) Hematocrit of the fluid leaving the capillaries is less than that of the fluid entering it.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "72728e29-52be-456a-a4ed-7b965689f8ba",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
