                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the given information\nThe patient has presented with three symptoms: flat topped violaceous papules, thinning of nails, and onycholysis. The question is asking about the microscopic finding observed in this patient.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the symptoms\nFlat topped violaceous papules are a characteristic feature of lichen planus, a chronic inflammatory skin condition. Thinning of nails and onycholysis (separation of the nail from the nail bed) can also be seen in lichen planus.\n\nStep 3: Understand the microscopic features of lichen planus\nLichen planus is characterized by a band-like lymphocytic infiltrate in the upper dermis, which can cause damage to the basal layer of the epidermis. This damage can result in a variety of microscopic findings, including basal cell degeneration, suprabasal split, and acantholysis.\n\nStep 4: Eliminate incorrect options\nOption A (prominent necrotic cell) is not a typical finding in lichen planus. Option C (basal cell degeneration) is a possible finding in lichen planus, but it is not specific to this condition. Option B (suprabasal split) is a finding that can be seen in lichen planus, but it is not the most specific or characteristic finding.\n\nStep 5: Choose the correct option\nOption D (acantholysis) is the most specific and characteristic microscopic finding in lichen planus. Acantholysis refers to the separation of epidermal cells from each other, resulting in the formation of intraepidermal clefts. This finding is seen in the majority of cases of lichen planus and is considered a hallmark of the condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Acantholysis.",
                        "answers": [
