                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nB) Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy\n\nThe sudden onset of severe bilateral vision loss, more so on one side, with no perception of light, suggests a vascular etiology. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) is a condition characterized by ischemia of the optic nerve head, leading to sudden vision loss, often more severe in one eye. The normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus suggest that the problem is localized to the optic nerve rather than the retina or brain. The ability to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests a visual field defect, which is commonly seen in AION. Optic neuritis and CMV retinitis typically present with other ocular findings, such as optic disc swelling or retinal lesions, respectively. Functional vision loss is a diagnosis of exclusion and should only be considered after ruling out organic causes of vision loss.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "7bed6dd6-6b75-44d9-a0d8-b9d674a0b703",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
