                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key features of the case.\n- Sudden severe bilateral loss of vision, more so on the right side\n- No perception of light\n- Normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus\n- Able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible diagnoses based on the key features.\n- Optic neuritis: This is inflammation of the optic nerve and can cause sudden vision loss, often with pain behind the eye. However, the normal pupillary reflexes and lack of pain make this less likely.\n- Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy: This is a blockage of blood flow to the optic nerve and can cause sudden vision loss, often in one eye. However, the fact that both eyes are affected makes this less likely.\n- CMV retinitis: This is a viral infection that can cause vision loss, but it typically affects people with weakened immune systems (such as those with HIV). The normal fundus exam makes this less likely.\n- Functional vision loss: This is when there is no physical cause for the vision loss and it is instead due to psychological factors. However, the fact that the patient is unable to touch the tips of her fingers with her left eye closed suggests a physical cause.\n\nStep 3: Choose the most likely diagnosis.\nBased on the key features and the above considerations, the most likely diagnosis is optic neuritis. However, it is important to note that further testing (such as MRI or visual evoked potentials) would be needed to confirm the diagnosis.",
                        "answers": [
