                        "cot": "\n\nThe sudden onset of severe bilateral vision loss with no perception of light suggests a problem with the optic nerve. Option A, optic neuritis, is a possibility, but the fact that the patient is able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests that the problem is more likely to be in the left optic nerve. Option B, anterior ischemic optic neuropathy, is a possibility, but it usually affects older individuals and is more commonly unilateral. Option C, CMV retinitis, is unlikely as it typically presents with gradual vision loss and is more common in immunocompromised individuals. Option D, functional vision loss, is also unlikely as the patient has no perception of light, which is not consistent with a functional disorder. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is A) Optic neuritis.",
