                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nD) Odorants. \n\nThe mammalian genome contains approximately 400-1000 genes that code for odorant receptors, which are responsible for detecting and distinguishing different odors. This is the largest family of receptors in the mammalian genome. The other options listed (immunoglobulin receptors, interleukins, and growth factors) are important in immune function and cell signaling, but they do not make up as large a proportion of the genome as odorant receptors.",
