                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what receptors are. Receptors are proteins that are located on the surface of cells and are responsible for receiving signals from outside the cell. These signals can be in the form of hormones, neurotransmitters, or other molecules that bind to the receptor and trigger a response inside the cell.\n\nNow, let's look at the answer choices:\n\nA) Immunoglobulin receptors: Immunoglobulins are antibodies that are produced by the immune system to help fight off infections. Immunoglobulin receptors are proteins that are located on the surface of immune cells and are responsible for binding to specific antigens (foreign substances) and triggering an immune response. While there are many different types of immunoglobulin receptors, they do not make up the majority of genes in the mammalian genome.\n\nB) Interleukins: Interleukins are a type of signaling molecule that are produced by immune cells and other cells in the body. They play a role in regulating the immune response and inflammation. While there are many different types of interleukins, they also do not make up the majority of genes in the mammalian genome.\n\nC) Growth factors: Growth factors are signaling molecules that are involved in cell growth, differentiation, and survival. They play a role in development, wound healing, and tissue repair. While there are many different types of growth factors, they also do not make up the majority of genes in the mammalian genome.\n\nD) Odorants: Odorants are molecules that are responsible for our sense of smell. Odorant receptors are proteins that are located on the surface of cells in the nose and are responsible for detecting different types of odorants. Interestingly, odorant receptors do make up a significant portion of the genes in the mammalian genome. In fact, it is estimated that there are over 1,000 different odorant receptor genes in the human genome.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Odorants.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "3583ef63-bdc2-4420-b7c1-9f32bf010c5f",
