                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to identify the wave patterns of EEF, ECG, and EMG. Unfortunately, the figure was not provided in the exam, so we cannot do that. However, we can make some assumptions based on the answer choices.\n\nNREM sleep is characterized by slow-wave sleep, which is reflected in the EEF as high-amplitude, low-frequency waves. The ECG and EMG may also show some changes during NREM sleep, but they are not specific to this stage.\n\nREM sleep, on the other hand, is characterized by rapid eye movements and a high level of brain activity, which is reflected in the EEF as low-amplitude, high-frequency waves. The ECG and EMG may also show some changes during REM sleep, such as muscle atonia and irregular heart rate.\n\nWakefulness is characterized by a high level of brain activity, which is reflected in the EEF as a mixture of high- and low-frequency waves. The ECG and EMG may also show some changes during wakefulness, such as a regular heart rate and muscle activity.\n\nQuiet wakefulness is a state of relaxed wakefulness, which is reflected in the EEF as a mixture of low- and high-frequency waves. The ECG and EMG may also show some changes during quiet wakefulness, such as a regular heart rate and reduced muscle activity.\n\nBased on these descriptions, we can conclude that the B pattern most likely belongs to REM sleep, as it is characterized by low-amplitude, high-frequency waves in the EEF. However, without the actual figure, we cannot be certain.",
