                        "cot": "\n\n1. EEF (electroencephalogram) measures brain activity and is used to determine sleep stages. In NREM sleep, the brain waves are slow and regular, while in REM sleep, they are fast and irregular. In wakefulness, the brain waves are fast and irregular.\n\n2. EMG (electromyogram) measures muscle activity and is used to determine muscle tone during sleep. In NREM sleep, muscle tone is high, while in REM sleep, it is low.\n\n3. ECG (electrocardiogram) measures heart activity and is used to determine heart rate and rhythm. In NREM sleep, heart rate and rhythm are slow and regular, while in REM sleep, they are fast and irregular.\n\n4. The B pattern is not provided, so we cannot use it to determine the answer.\n\n5. Based on the wave patterns of EEF, EMG, and ECG, we can conclude that the pattern belongs to REM sleep, as the brain waves are fast and irregular, muscle tone is low, and heart rate and rhythm are fast and irregular.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B) REM sleep.",
