                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, the patient presents with symptoms of hydrophobia, tearing, and altered sensorium, which are consistent with rabies. \n\nNext, a corneal impression has been taken. This is a diagnostic technique used to detect the presence of the rabies virus in the patient's body. \n\nThe most accurate test to perform on the corneal impression for the diagnosis of rabies is C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies. Negri bodies are eosinophilic, intracytoplasmic inclusions found in the neurons of infected animals and humans. They are considered pathognomonic for rabies virus infection. \n\nA) Indirect immunofluorescence and D) Antibodies against Rabies virus are also diagnostic tests for rabies, but they are not as accurate as histopathological examination for Negri bodies. \n\nB) PCR for virus can also be used to detect the presence of the rabies virus, but it is not typically performed on corneal impressions. Instead, it is usually performed on samples of saliva, cerebrospinal fluid, or brain tissue.",
                        "answers": [
