                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is showing symptoms of rabies, so we need to confirm the diagnosis. Corneal impression has been taken, which means we have a sample to work with. \n\nOption A, indirect immunofluorescence, is a test that detects antibodies against the rabies virus. This test is useful for confirming a previous exposure to the virus, but it may not be accurate in the early stages of the disease. \n\nOption B, PCR for virus, is a test that detects the genetic material of the virus. This test is highly sensitive and specific, and it can detect the virus even in the early stages of the disease. \n\nOption C, histopathological examination for Negri bodies, is a test that looks for characteristic structures in the brain tissue of infected animals. This test is not commonly used in humans, as it requires a brain biopsy, which is invasive and risky. \n\nOption D, antibodies against Rabies virus, is similar to option A and detects antibodies against the virus. \n\nBased on this information, the most accurate test for diagnosing rabies in this patient would be option B, PCR for virus.",
