                        "cot": "A) Indirect immunofluorescence: This test can be used to detect rabies virus antigens in corneal impressions, but it may not be the most accurate method.\n\nB) PCR for virus: Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a highly sensitive and specific method for detecting viral nucleic acids, making it a more accurate diagnostic tool for rabies.\n\nC) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies: Negri bodies are intracytoplasmic inclusions found in the neurons of rabies-infected animals. However, their presence is not always consistent, and their absence does not rule out rabies infection.\n\nD) Antibodies against Rabies virus: Detection of antibodies against the rabies virus can be helpful in diagnosing the disease, but it may not be the most accurate method, especially in the early stages of infection.\n\nBased on the information above, the most accurate diagnostic test for rabies in this case would be PCR for the virus (option B).",
