                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is D) Eruptive stage until 7th day of rash.\n\nMeasles is a highly contagious viral infection that spreads through respiratory droplets. It is important to isolate infected individuals to prevent the spread of the disease. The isolation period for measles is determined by the duration of the infectious stage, which is from the onset of prodromal symptoms to the end of the rash.\n\nThe prodromal stage is the initial phase of the disease, characterized by non-specific symptoms such as fever, cough, runny nose, and red eyes. The eruptive stage is when the characteristic rash appears, starting on the face and spreading to the rest of the body.\n\nOption A) states that the isolation period is from the onset of prodromal stage until 7th day of rash. This is incorrect because the infectious period ends at the end of the rash, which can last up to 10 days. Therefore, isolating individuals until the 7th day of rash would not be sufficient to prevent the spread of the disease.\n\nOption B) states that the isolation period is from the eruptive stage until 2 days of rash. This is incorrect because the infectious period lasts longer than 2 days of rash. Isolating individuals for only 2 days of rash would not be sufficient to prevent the spread of the disease.\n\nOption C) states that the isolation period is from the onset of prodromal phase until 3rd day of rash. This is incorrect because the infectious period lasts longer than 3 days of rash. Isolating individuals for only 3 days of rash would not be sufficient to prevent the spread of the disease.\n\nOption D) states that the isolation period is from the eruptive stage until 7th day of rash. This is the correct answer because the infectious period ends at the end of the rash, which can last up to 10 days. Isolating individuals until the 7th day of rash would be sufficient to prevent the spread of the disease.",
