                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understanding the given information\nThe given information states that the patient has jaundice with 50% direct bilirubin, and all other liver function tests (LFTs) are normal. Based on this information, we need to identify the correct diagnosis from the given options.\n\nStep 2: Understanding the options\nLet's understand the given options to identify the correct diagnosis:\n\nA) Rotor syndrome: It is a rare genetic disorder that causes elevated levels of bilirubin in the blood, leading to jaundice. However, in Rotor syndrome, both direct and indirect bilirubin levels are elevated.\n\nB) Gilbert syndrome: It is a common genetic disorder that causes mild jaundice due to the reduced ability of the liver to process bilirubin. In Gilbert syndrome, indirect bilirubin levels are elevated, while direct bilirubin levels are normal.\n\nC) Glucuronyl transferase deficiency: It is a rare genetic disorder that causes elevated levels of indirect bilirubin due to the reduced ability of the liver to conjugate bilirubin.\n\nD) Primary biliary cirrhosis: It is a chronic liver disease that causes damage to the bile ducts, leading to elevated levels of bilirubin in the blood. However, in primary biliary cirrhosis, both direct and indirect bilirubin levels are elevated.\n\nStep 3: Analyzing the options\nBased on the given information, we know that the patient has 50% direct bilirubin, which rules out options A, C, and D. These conditions are characterized by elevated levels of indirect bilirubin or both direct and indirect bilirubin.\n\nThe only option left is B, Gilbert syndrome, which is characterized by elevated levels of indirect bilirubin and normal levels of direct bilirubin. Therefore, the correct diagnosis in this case is Gilbert syndrome.\n\nStep 4: Conclusion\nBased on the given information, we can conclude that the patient's diagnosis is Gilbert syndrome, which is characterized by mild jaundice with elevated levels of indirect bilirubin and normal levels of direct bilirubin.",
                        "answers": [
