                    {
                        "id": "3da4a118-1205-47ff-b4e2-adb581f6df5b",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-09",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nJaundice is a condition where there is an excess of bilirubin in the blood, leading to yellowing of the skin and eyes. Bilirubin is a waste product that is produced when red blood cells are broken down. It is normally processed by the liver and excreted in the bile. \n\nThere are two types of bilirubin \u2013 direct (conjugated) and indirect (unconjugated). Direct bilirubin is water-soluble and can be excreted in the urine, while indirect bilirubin is fat-soluble and needs to be processed by the liver before it can be excreted. \n\nIn this case, the patient has 50% direct bilirubin, which means that there is a problem with the liver's ability to process bilirubin. This rules out Gilbert syndrome, which is a genetic condition where there is a deficiency of the enzyme that converts indirect bilirubin to direct bilirubin. \n\nRotor syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that causes a similar type of jaundice, but it is characterized by high levels of both direct and indirect bilirubin. \n\nGlucuronyl transferase deficiency is another rare genetic disorder that causes jaundice, but it is characterized by high levels of indirect bilirubin. \n\nPrimary biliary cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease that can cause jaundice, but it is usually associated with elevated levels of other liver enzymes, such as alkaline phosphatase and gamma-glutamyl transferase. \n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis in this case is Rotor syndrome.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b0bcfd54-1685-40f2-99cd-8d44d032c5de",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Rotor syndrome.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/16 19:28:06",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
