                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with symptoms and signs suggestive of acute viral hepatitis. The LFTs show elevated bilirubin and liver enzymes. To confirm the diagnosis and determine the specific viral etiology, the following investigations should be sent:\n\nA) HBsAg, IgM antiHBC, AntiHCV AntiHEV\n\nThis option includes testing for hepatitis B, C, and E viruses, as well as recent infection with hepatitis B (IgM antiHBC). This is a reasonable choice, but it does not include testing for hepatitis A virus, which is a common cause of acute viral hepatitis and should be included in the initial workup.\n\nB) AntiHAV, HBsAg, IgM antiHBc, AntiHCV\n\nThis option includes testing for hepatitis A, B, and C viruses, as well as recent infection with hepatitis B (IgM antiHBc). This is a better choice than option A, as it includes testing for hepatitis A virus, which is likely the cause of the patient's symptoms.\n\nC) HBsAg, IgM antiHBC,AntiHDV AntiHCV AntiHEV\n\nThis option includes testing for hepatitis B, C, and E viruses, as well as recent infection with hepatitis B (IgM antiHBC) and hepatitis D virus. However, hepatitis D virus is only found in patients with hepatitis B, and the patient's HBsAg status is not known. Therefore, this option is not necessary and may lead to unnecessary testing.\n\nD) AntiHAV IgMantiHBC, AntiHCV, AntiHEV.\n\nThis option includes testing for hepatitis A, B, and C viruses, as well as recent infection with hepatitis B (IgM antiHBC) and hepatitis E virus. This is a reasonable choice, as it includes testing for all the common causes of acute viral hepatitis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) AntiHAV IgMantiHBC, AntiHCV, AntiHEV.",
