                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is a 40-year-old male who is a known smoker and presents with fever, fatigue, jaundice, yellow colored urine, and clay colored stools. He has also developed an aversion to cigarette smoking. On examination, scleral icterus is present. The LFT results show a total bilirubin of 18.5, direct bilirubin of 7.5, SGOT of 723, and SGPT of 812. \n\nThese symptoms and LFT results suggest that the patient may have acute viral hepatitis. To rule out this possibility, the following investigations should be sent:\n\nOption A: HBsAg, IgM antiHBC, AntiHCV AntiHEV\nThis option includes tests for hepatitis B, C, and E viruses, as well as IgM antiHBC, which is an antibody produced during acute hepatitis B infection. This option is a good choice for ruling out acute viral hepatitis.\n\nOption B: AntiHAV, HBsAg, IgM antiHBc, AntiHCV\nThis option includes tests for hepatitis A, B, and C viruses, as well as IgM antiHBc, which is an antibody produced during acute hepatitis B infection. This option is also a good choice for ruling out acute viral hepatitis.\n\nOption C: HBsAg, IgM antiHBC, AntiHDV AntiHCV AntiHEV\nThis option includes tests for hepatitis B, C, and E viruses, as well as IgM antiHBC and AntiHDV, which is an antibody produced during acute hepatitis D infection. However, hepatitis D is rare and usually occurs in people who are already infected with hepatitis B. Therefore, this option may not be necessary in this case.\n\nOption D: AntiHAV IgMantiHBC, AntiHCV, AntiHEV\nThis option includes tests for hepatitis A, B, and C viruses, as well as IgM antiHBC, which is an antibody produced during acute hepatitis B infection. However, this option does not include a test for hepatitis E virus, which is a common cause of acute viral hepatitis in India. Therefore, this option may not be the best choice for ruling out acute viral hepatitis in this case.\n\nIn conclusion, options A and B are both good choices for ruling out acute viral hepatitis in this patient. Option C may not be necessary, and option D may not be the best choice due to the lack of a test for hepatitis E virus.",
                        "answers": [
