                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe correct answer is C) Ectoderm and mesoderm from epiblast; endoderm from hypoblast.\n\nHere's why:\n\nDuring embryonic development, the blastocyst (a hollow ball of cells) implants into the uterine wall and begins to differentiate into different cell types. The blastocyst has two main layers: the inner cell mass (ICM) and the outer layer of cells called the trophoblast.\n\nThe ICM eventually gives rise to the embryo proper, while the trophoblast forms the placenta and other supporting tissues.\n\nWithin the ICM, there are two distinct populations of cells: the epiblast and the hypoblast. The epiblast is located closer to the amniotic cavity (the fluid-filled sac that surrounds the developing embryo), while the hypoblast is closer to the blastocyst cavity.\n\nAs the embryo develops, the epiblast gives rise to the ectoderm (which forms the skin, nervous system, and other tissues), as well as the mesoderm (which forms the muscles, bones, and other connective tissues).\n\nMeanwhile, the hypoblast gives rise to the endoderm (which forms the lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts, as well as other internal organs).\n\nTherefore, the 3 embryonic germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm) are derived from both the epiblast and the hypoblast, with the ectoderm and mesoderm coming from the epiblast and the endoderm coming from the hypoblast.",
