                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nC) Corticosteroids\n\nThis presentation is suggestive of acute epiglottitis, which is a medical emergency. The primary management is securing the airway, but in addition to that, corticosteroids are recommended to reduce inflammation and edema of the epiglottis. Nebulized racemic epinephrine may be used in croup, but it is not indicated in epiglottitis. Anti-diphtheria toxin is not indicated in this case as the presentation is not suggestive of diphtheria. IV ceftriaxone may be used to cover for potential bacterial infections, but it is not the primary treatment for acute epiglottitis.",
                        "answers": [
