                    {
                        "id": "a99459fb-a359-48d5-ab05-face99eaea28",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe presentation of high grade fever, inspiratory stridor, and swallowing difficulty with drooling of saliva suggests a possible diagnosis of acute epiglottitis, which is a medical emergency. The first step in management is to secure the airway, which involves intubation or tracheostomy if necessary. Once the airway is secured, the following treatment options can be considered:\n\nA) Nebulized racemic epinephrine: This is not recommended as the primary treatment for acute epiglottitis. It may be used as an adjunctive therapy to reduce airway edema, but it should not delay definitive airway management.\n\nB) Anti-diphtheria toxin: This is not indicated in the management of acute epiglottitis. Diphtheria is a rare cause of epiglottitis, and the diagnosis can be confirmed by laboratory tests.\n\nC) Corticosteroids: This is recommended as an adjunctive therapy to reduce airway edema and inflammation. Dexamethasone is the preferred corticosteroid, given as a single dose of 0.6 mg/kg IV or IM.\n\nD) IV ceftriaxone: This is recommended as the first-line antibiotic therapy for acute epiglottitis. Other options include ampicillin-sulbactam or a second- or third-generation cephalosporin.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Corticosteroids.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "741398ba-891d-46f9-9df7-8d9195188e4b",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
